{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T22:29:49.465Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a192303f8349f847a</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01GgiDycispAVroBVsvCSixb</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/a192303f8349f847a.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Implement amended TASK-3\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Committed as `6e72ead0`.\n\n## Summary\n\n**TASK-3** completed successfully with one genuine finding along the way.\n\n- Confirmed clean baseline: 6+ commits from TASK-1/2/4/5/7/8 present on `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs`, working tree clean.\n- Read AC-002b and AC-007 verbatim from `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK2-PLATFORM-MANIFEST-WRITES.json` before writing tests.\n- Wrote the plan's exact AC-002b test code verbatim — it passed immediately (no implementation change needed, as predicted), confirming TASK-2's loop hardcodes `ManifestRole::Canonical` and correctly resolves Cargo workspace-inherited versions.\n- For AC-007, the plan's `test.code` field only contained the AC-002b test — the AC-007 test had to be authored from the description. My first attempt (two separate walk-discovered directories) genuinely failed red (`platform_writes.len() == 0`), and investigation of `walk.rs:131-227` showed why: platform-manifest `owner` resolution is strictly per-directory (Case D co-location), so a directory without a co-located Cargo.toml resolves `owner` to itself — meaning two real, disk-discovered platform siblings under one true owner are not representable through current walk.rs discovery at all (the same npm-only/single-file constraint AC-002b's correction already documents for Cargo).\n- Checked the actual spec text for AC-007: it names no discovery mechanism, only \"a Fixed group whose owner has two GroupMember::PlatformManifest siblings.\" The \"real, disk-discovered\" framing was only in the plan's narrative, not the spec criterion itself. I rewrote the test to use one real Case D sibling plus one fixture-injected sibling (same technique TASK-3 already sanctions for AC-002b), which faithfully tests what the spec actually requires — the loop iterates every platform member, not just the first — without falsely asserting a disk-discovery capability that doesn't exist.\n- `cargo fmt -p callisto-graph`: clean. `cargo clippy -p callisto-graph --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean. `cargo test -p callisto-graph --lib`: 375 passed, 0 failed.\n- Committed as `6e72ead0`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"TASK-3\",\n  \"status\": \"done\",\n  \"steps_completed\": [\n    \"Verified clean git tree and prior commits present\",\n    \"Read AC-002b and AC-007 verbatim from spec file\",\n    \"Added plan_version_platform_write_from_resolves_cargo_workspace_inherited_version (plan's exact test code) - passed immediately, no implementation change\",\n    \"Authored plan_version_emits_platform_write_for_each_of_two_platform_siblings from description - initial version genuinely failed red due to a real walk.rs discovery limitation, revised to fixture-inject the second sibling matching what the spec text actually requires\",\n    \"cargo fmt -p callisto-graph\",\n    \"cargo clippy -p callisto-graph --all-targets -- -D warnings (clean)\",\n    \"cargo test -p callisto-graph --lib (375 passed, 0 failed)\",\n    \"Committed\"\n  ],\n  \"test_result\": \"pass\",\n  \"commit_sha\": \"6e72ead0\",\n  \"criteria_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-002b\",\n      \"test_file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs\",\n      \"test_line\": 840,\n      \"implementation_file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs\",\n      \"implementation_line\": 282\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-007\",\n      \"test_file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs\",\n      \"test_line\": 980,\n      \"implementation_file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs\",\n      \"implementation_line\": 282\n    }\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": \"The plan's reasoning/task description for AC-007 characterized both siblings as 'real, disk-discovered' -- empirically this is not achievable: walk.rs's platform-manifest discovery (walk.rs:131-227) resolves `owner` strictly from the co-located Cargo.toml in the SAME directory as the npm package.json (Case D), and a directory holds at most one package.json, so a second sibling under the same true owner cannot be disk-discovered today. The actual spec criterion text for AC-007 (read from disk) makes no claim about discovery mechanism, so I wrote the test with one real Case D sibling and one fixture-injected sibling (the same construction technique the plan itself sanctions for AC-002b), which proves exactly what the spec's literal text requires -- multiple platform members each get their own PlatformWrite -- without asserting a disk-discovery capability that doesn't exist. Recommend flagging this to vector-planner/canon so AC-007's plan-level narrative (not the spec text itself) gets a similar clarifying note to AC-002b's, to avoid a future implementer being confused by the same dead end.\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Verified spec text directly before writing tests. AC-002b test matched plan's exact code and passed immediately as predicted (no impl change). AC-007's description-only test required original authorship; the first construction attempt (two independently walk-discovered sibling directories) was a genuine red failure caused by a real architectural constraint in walk.rs, not a missing implementation feature -- confirmed by reading walk.rs's directory-scoped owner-resolution loop and the existing 'real_platform_manifest_resolves_via_fixed_group_and_feeds_napi_drift' Case-D test. Since the spec criterion itself (not the plan's paraphrase) makes no disk-discovery claim, used fixture injection for the second sibling, consistent with AC-002b's own sanctioned approach, avoiding a spec_contradiction while still proving the literal criterion.\"\n}\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>104664</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>31</tool_uses><duration_ms>386638</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
