{"parentUuid":null,"logicalParentUuid":"4cbf5f21-9d7a-47f8-94b8-083ad9b6607a","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"compact_boundary","content":"Conversation compacted","level":"info","compactMetadata":{"trigger":"auto","preTokens":934875,"postTokens":23779,"cumulativeDroppedTokens":6972224,"durationMs":167128,"preCompactDiscoveredTools":["DesignSync","EnterPlanMode","ExitPlanMode","Monitor","PushNotification","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch"],"preservedSegment":{"headUuid":"66648324-2b89-4134-8594-7a308a086a68","anchorUuid":"0892eba7-4794-4223-a34c-316f2f28503b","tailUuid":"4cbf5f21-9d7a-47f8-94b8-083ad9b6607a"},"preservedMessages":{"anchorUuid":"0892eba7-4794-4223-a34c-316f2f28503b","uuids":["66648324-2b89-4134-8594-7a308a086a68","bb7ff0c2-38f9-4949-a6d9-67f4f8925b88","ad36d2f6-349e-4784-b2b0-b6b9d9ffec83","bae1c929-00a6-48c0-9f3a-3bc69ebbb5fd","4cbf5f21-9d7a-47f8-94b8-083ad9b6607a"],"allUuids":["66648324-2b89-4134-8594-7a308a086a68","bb7ff0c2-38f9-4949-a6d9-67f4f8925b88","bc0e95ed-f1c8-4c63-b223-27bba583ad74","ad36d2f6-349e-4784-b2b0-b6b9d9ffec83","bae1c929-00a6-48c0-9f3a-3bc69ebbb5fd","089dc445-6837-43bb-bfd7-e58e51a060fe","4cbf5f21-9d7a-47f8-94b8-083ad9b6607a"]}},"uuid":"34d11b63-eede-48d8-9ac3-75e217d4056c","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:00:10.290Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
{"parentUuid":"34d11b63-eede-48d8-9ac3-75e217d4056c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3252d725-4672-4611-bb9c-1220c6560ac4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   This is a continuation of an extremely long-running systematic remediation session for the \"callisto\" Rust monorepo (a release/versioning tool), executing a 12-track remediation plan derived from an earlier 144-finding adversarial audit. Explicit user directives in this window, in chronological order:\n   - After a session interruption (user hit weekly API limit; another agent \"agy\" continued work meanwhile): \"Can you check the current state vs the plan?\" — I audited and found Track3a/3b1 done, additional uncommitted work, and Track 2 only ~20% done.\n   - When I found Track 2 depended on Track 1, offered options via AskUserQuestion; user chose \"Do Track 1 first, then Track 2 (Recommended).\"\n   - \"keep going with expediency but keeping the highest level of quality and keeping our token usage in mind\" — the standing directive governing pacing/rigor tradeoffs for the rest of the session.\n   - Periodic check-ins (\"What happened??\", \"Are you stuck??\", \"Is the exit gate ready?\", \"Are we not using the new plugins that should be doing better at this?\") — all addressed with direct status explanations, not new work directions.\n   - \"How much of our audit have we addressed?\" — answered with a full 12-track status table.\n   - \"We were only 20% done track 2 this morning. Why has that not moved at all????\" — I explained the Track 1 dependency was the reason, per their own earlier choice; user replied \"Ok, keep going with expediency but keeping the highest level of quality and keeping our token usage in mind\" (restated the standing directive).\n   - \"How do we unblock it?\" (re: the two blocking master-plan decisions, Track 3a's match semantics and Track 4/6's wire-format versioning).\n   - For decision 1: \"For #1 we need a simple config and unique naming patterns. I was thinking the name in the config and the publish method are unique enough to match right?\"\n   - For decision 2: \"I need more details on decision 2 to help here. Seems like the GH action should be mostly calling the cli and acting on it right? less parsing?\"\n   - Via AskUserQuestion (after research): chose \"Bump schemaVersion for MatrixReport + the plan-publish report; correctness-fix the rest (Recommended).\"\n   - \"What's next? Do it\" — directed me to pick and execute the next logical work item (I chose Track 9 per master-plan phasing).\n   - Via AskUserQuestion (re: Track 9 bug 2's discovered scope): chose \"Full canon pipeline, same rigor as Track 1 (Recommended).\"\n   - \"Didn't we fix all the retrying???\" — I explained the exit-gate retries are genuine, not a bug; offered to skip rigor if preferred.\n   - \"Keep going then\" — confirmed continuing the full-rigor pipeline as-is.\n   - Most recently: a system-issued mid-turn interruption message (\"What happened??\") appeared attached to a tool result, which I is addressed inline, followed immediately by the system-level compaction-summary request I am now responding to.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Canon → Vector → Lambda SDD pipeline: canon-drafter → canon-verifier → canon-auditor → canon-exit-gate (spec gating, retry_count-based escalation past 3) → orchestrator writes spec to `.claude/specs/`, commits → vector-planner → vector-challenger (plan gating) → orchestrator writes plan to `.claude/plans/`, commits → lambda-recon → lambda-implementer (strict TDD: red→green→commit, one task at a time) → lambda-reviewer (read-only) → lambda-mutator (mutation testing) → lambda-exit-gate (final adversarial gate).\n   - `canon/correct` mode: when a lambda-implementer finds a genuine spec-vs-reality contradiction, the affected criterion is revised (not faked around) and the corrected spec re-enters verify→audit→gate before overwriting the file on disk at the same path with `revision_note` set; the plan is then amended via `vector-planner`'s amend mode for just the affected task(s).\n   - Layer isolation invariant (CLAUDE.md): `callisto-model` (Layer 1) must stay permissive (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and never depend on Layer 2 crates (`callisto-graph` etc.) or their types — this specifically blocked a naive fix for Track 9 bug 2 (`IdentityIndex`, a `callisto-graph` type, cannot be exposed through the `DependencyResolver` trait defined in `callisto-model`).\n   - `unsafe_code = \"forbid\"` workspace invariant, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` mandatory CI, format-check pre-commit hook (fails on unformatted commits — fix is `cargo fmt` + re-stage + fresh commit, never amend).\n   - Generic-vs-concrete-type compile boundary: `plan_version<R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver, I: SeverityInference>` and `plan_snapshot<R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver>` are generic over `D`; `ManifestWalkResolver::identity() -> &IdentityIndex` is an *inherent* method (not part of the `DependencyResolver` trait), so it cannot be called from generic function bodies regardless of what concrete type `D` resolves to at any real call site — monomorphization at call sites does not make inherent methods visible inside a generic function's own body. `Workspace::load`, by contrast, is defined only in the non-generic `impl<'a, R: CommandRunner> Workspace<'a, R, ManifestWalkResolver>` block, so `graph.identity()` genuinely is callable there.\n   - Established \"regression-proof, no-red-step\" pattern from Track 1/2: many plan tasks are pure regression-pinning tests for already-correct code; implementers are expected to verify non-vacuity by temporarily breaking the underlying logic, confirming the new test fails, then restoring it — this pattern was used dozens of times successfully.\n   - `PackagePattern` (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/pattern.rs): matches by package NAME glob with an optional `ecosystem:` prefix for disambiguation (`\"cargo:pkg-*\"` vs `\"npm:pkg-*\"`); a bare pattern ambiguous across ecosystems triggers `ConfigError::BareRuleMatchesMultipleEcosystems`.\n   - `IdentityIndex::native_name(&self, id: &PackageId, eco: Ecosystem) -> Option<&str>` (crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs:493) — the correct ecosystem-native-name lookup that Track 9 bug 2 needed but `solve_cascade` had no access to.\n   - `RewriteKey`/`SpecRewrite`/`CascadeOutcome.rewrites` (crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs) — the dependency-spec-rewrite bookkeeping structures at the center of Track 9 bug 2.\n   - `CascadeInput<'a, D: DependencyResolver>` — struct that has grown fields across tracks: `graph, groups, cfg, seed, reasons, named_by, base, pre` (original), `+tags: &'a TagIndex` (Track 1), and now (Track 9, spec'd but not yet implemented) `+identity: &'a IdentityIndex`.\n   - `Workspace<'a, R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver = ManifestWalkResolver>` (crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs) — will gain a new `identity: IdentityIndex` field per Track 9's spec (IdentityIndex derives `Clone`), populated once inside `Workspace::load`'s existing non-generic impl block.\n   - GitHub Action JSON contract: only two `jq` sites actually parse callisto's own JSON output (`MatrixReport.platformTargets[].targets[].artifactName`; `.releases[].tagName` from `plan-publish`); everything else (`status --check`, `publish`, `tag`, `git push`) is exit-code/CLI-invocation based — this materially reduces the real risk/scope of Track 4/6-mechanical's wire-format-versioning decision.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `.claude/plans/ACTIVE.md` — master tracking doc; updated and committed after every completed track (Track 3a/3b1, Track 6-doc/8-doc, Track 1, Track 2, Track 3a-match-semantics, mutation-testing correction) with full exit-gate-round history. Most recent commits touching it: `9ccea10` (initial, later-corrected Track 1 mutation note), `94cc140` (correction to reflect the *completed* mutation run: 113 mutants, 42 caught, 28 survivors all in pre-existing/untouched code, zero in Track 1's own new code — the earlier note was based on a mid-run snapshot), `4aa4a2d` (Track 2 DONE), `eb5073c` (Track 3a DONE).\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs` — central file for Track 1 (already landed, Fixed-group convergence block) and Track 9. This session directly edited it for Track 9 bug 1:\n     ```rust\n     pub fn cascade_action(\n         kind: DepKind,\n         coverage: Coverage,\n         source: Severity,   // renamed from _source\n         cfg: &CascadeConfig,\n     ) -> CascadeDecision {\n         ...\n         (Peer, DoesNotCover)\n             if cfg.peer_escalation\n                 && matches!(coverage, DoesNotCover)\n                 && matches!(source, Severity::Minor | Severity::Major) =>\n         {\n             (Severity::Major, Some(ConfigKey::CASCADE_PEER_ESCALATION), true)\n         }\n         (Peer, DoesNotCover) => (\n             cfg.bump_severity.as_severity(),\n             Some(ConfigKey::CASCADE_BUMP_SEVERITY),\n             false,\n         ),\n     ```\n     Added test `cascade_action_peer_escalation_only_fires_for_non_patch_source` (Patch/Minor/Major cases, later extended with a `Severity::None` case per bug-hunter review suggestion). Committed as `a7007d9`. The RewriteKey-construction bug (bug 2, around line ~259-283, `if d.rewrite { ... let key = RewriteKey { ..., name: edge.to.name().to_string(), ... } }`) is spec'd but NOT yet implemented.\n   - `.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK9-CASCADE-REWRITE-KEY-CORRECTNESS.json` — newly created and committed (`fea63de`) this session. 7 acceptance criteria (AC-001 through AC-007). Key api_surface entries: `CascadeInput.identity` (`pub identity: &'a IdentityIndex`), `Workspace.identity` (`pub identity: IdentityIndex`, populated in `Workspace::load`'s non-generic impl block), and the corrected rewrite-key construction: `name: input.identity.native_name(&edge.to, eco).map(str::to_string).unwrap_or_else(|| edge.to.name().to_string()),`. Scope explicitly enumerates 18 hand-constructed `Workspace` struct literals across 6 test files that must each gain `identity: IdentityIndex::default()`: `tests/validate_command_tests.rs` (lines 112, 180), `tests/snapshot_tests.rs` (70, 132, 183, 255), `tests/matrix_tests.rs` (63, 101), `tests/graph_tests.rs` (35), `tests/tags_tests.rs` (62, 172, 333, 494), `tests/publish_tests.rs` (167, 679, 742, 974, 1342) — plus 14 `CascadeInput` test-literal sites (8 in cascade.rs's own test module, 6 in `tests/cascade_tests.rs`) and 2 real construction sites (`commands/version.rs:73`, `commands/snapshot.rs:54`).\n   - `docs/00-design.md` — corrected this session (commit `b190626`) for Track 3a: worked example `match = \"crates/*\"` → `\"cargo:*\"`; `\"packages/*\"` → `\"npm:*\"`; `\"packages/special-case\"` → `\"special-case\"`; new explanatory paragraph inserted after \"Discovery walks the workspace...\" explaining `match` is name+ecosystem-prefix based, not path-based, and is a deliberately different axis from `[[fixed-group]]`/`[[linked-group]]` `members`.\n   - `docs/01-spec.md` — corrected this session (same commit `b190626`): §14's formal pseudocode algorithm rewritten from `for each discovered package P (by project root path, workspace-relative)` / `whose match == P` to name-glob-matching semantics with a new explanatory note; `BareRuleMatchesMultipleEcosystems`'s doc comment typo fixed (`cargo/name` slash → `cargo:name` colon).\n   - `.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK1-FIXED-GROUP-CASCADE-CORRECTNESS.json`, `.claude/plans/PLAN-TRACK1-FIXED-GROUP-CASCADE-CORRECTNESS.json` — Track 1's fully-shipped spec/plan (12 tasks T01-T12, all landed, commits `bb9d3aa` through `a7e5526` per earlier phase — wait, correction: Track 1's task commits were `bb9d3aa` through `a7e5526` is actually Track 2's range; Track 1's were an earlier distinct set already committed before this summary's start — not fully re-enumerable here but confirmed DONE with `just ci` green and clean mutation testing).\n   - `.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK2-PLATFORM-MANIFEST-WRITES.json`, `.claude/plans/PLAN-TRACK2-PLATFORM-MANIFEST-WRITES.json` — Track 2's fully-shipped spec/plan (8 tasks). Key commits: `5eb5937` (T1, PlatformWrite.from field), `431feb9` (T2), `a309ff7` (T7), `4726863` (T8), `c3516ba` (T5), `1d360d5`+`f76088e` (AC-002b correction + plan amend), `6e72ead0` (amended T3), `ef1c633` (T6, last task). `2439832` = spec commit, `262cc43` = plan commit.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/config/pattern.rs` — read (not modified) to confirm `PackagePattern`'s existing name+ecosystem matching mechanism, central evidence for the Track 3a decision.\n   - `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml` — read (via fork research) to confirm the Action's minimal real JSON-parsing surface, central evidence for the Track 4/6-mechanical wire-format decision.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **Untracked/uncommitted already-approved work discovered repeatedly**: Multiple times this session, gated specs, plans, and doc-only fixes existed only in the working tree (from \"agy\"'s work while tokens were unavailable, and later from my own subagents). Fixed each time by carefully splitting diffs by hunk (using Python-based unified-diff hunk extraction when a single file mixed unrelated concerns) and committing each concern separately, never batching unrelated changes.\n   - **Premature \"waiting for X\" subagent notifications** (recurring throughout, both `lambda:implementer` and `canon:drafter`): the agent's process either genuinely continued in the background (confirmed via `ListAgents` showing \"running\") or had genuinely ended with uncommitted work-in-progress. Fix: check `git status`/`git diff` directly; if real, correct work exists uncommitted, verify it (run the specific test, then fmt/clippy/full suite) and commit it myself; if the agent needs to actually do the work, re-dispatch with an explicit \"do the work yourself in this invocation, do not describe a plan or delegate\" instruction — this fixed `canon:drafter` twice (Track 2's AC-002b correction, Track 9's initial draft).\n   - **Track 1 mutation-testing report was initially wrong**: A `lambda:mutator` agent's process ended before `cargo-mutants` finished; I treated this as `tool_unavailable`/incomplete per protocol and recorded it as a non-blocking gap in `ACTIVE.md`. Much later, the `lambda:exit-gate` agent (which had also seemed to end prematurely but was actually still alive) returned a delayed final report showing it had *waited for the actual cargo-mutants process to finish* and found the true result: zero survivors in Track 1's own new code (all 28 survivors were in pre-existing, untouched decision logic). I corrected `ACTIVE.md`'s earlier note (commit `94cc140`) rather than leaving the stale, worse characterization in place.\n   - **Track 2's AC-002b was a genuine spec-vs-reality contradiction**: TASK-3's implementer found that no Cargo-format manifest can become a `GroupMember::PlatformManifest` through real disk discovery (`walk.rs:199` gates platform-manifest registration to `Ecosystem::Npm` only). Correctly stopped rather than faking a test. Fixed via `canon/correct` (2 rounds): narrowed AC-002b to verify the OpenContext/workspace-inheritance resolution logic via a *fixture-constructed* `GroupMember::PlatformManifest` (bypassing walk.rs discovery), added a non_goals entry deferring the Cargo-discovery extension. Round 1 of the correction's own exit-gate found 3 more issues (vacuous assertion missing the owner-bump precondition; unfalsifiable role-forwarding clause; stale `purpose` claim) — all fixed directly.\n   - **A second, related discovery limitation surfaced in TASK-3/TASK-6** (Track 2): `walk.rs`'s platform-manifest discovery is directory-scoped (Case D: owner+platform manifest must be co-located), so two platform siblings under one true owner can never both be disk-discovered. Judged this did NOT require a further spec correction, since the *spec's own criterion text* for AC-007 never claimed disk-discovery — only the *plan's* narrative did. The implementer used one real Case D sibling + one fixture-injected sibling (the same technique established for AC-002b), and this was accepted as correct without another correction cycle.\n   - **Track 9's original master-plan tier assignment (\"lighter TDD loop, narrow single-function\") was wrong for bug 2**: direct investigation (and a dispatched fork) found the fix genuinely requires `CascadeInput`/`Workspace` field-threading comparable to Track 1's `tags` field, touching ~16-32 call sites, due to the Layer 1/Layer 2 isolation invariant blocking a naive fix. Surfaced to the user via AskUserQuestion rather than silently under- or over-scoping; user chose full canon pipeline.\n   - **Track 9 spec drafting error — CRITICAL, found by canon-auditor**: The first spec draft (and a subsequent verifier pass that failed to catch it) proposed using `ws.graph.identity()` directly at `plan_version`/`plan_snapshot`'s `CascadeInput` construction sites as a \"simplification\" over adding a new `Workspace.identity` field — but this does NOT compile, since both functions are generic over `D: DependencyResolver` and `identity()` is only an inherent method on the concrete `ManifestWalkResolver`, not part of the trait bound. The auditor correctly identified this was the SAME correct reasoning as `Workspace::load` (which genuinely is non-generic) *incorrectly extended* to two different, generic functions. Fixed by reverting to the requirement's original design: a genuine new owned `Workspace.identity: IdentityIndex` field. Exit-gate retry 1 then found two more real, mechanical gaps from this same fix (18 unaccounted-for `Workspace` struct literals across 6 test files that would also break; AC-004's exception clause became self-contradictory once those were considered) — both fixed directly via Python JSON patches. Exit-gate retry 2 PASSED with the reviewing agent independently re-deriving and confirming all 18 file:line citations via a fresh `grep`.\n   - **User pushback on `AskUserQuestion` usage**: My first attempt to resolve the two blocking decisions used two abstract, under-informed questions; the user rejected the tool call and said they wanted to clarify first. I then did direct code research (via a fork) before re-asking, which produced much better-grounded, narrower questions the user could actually answer confidently. Lesson (not saved as a formal memory in this window, but should be treated as standing guidance): don't reach for `AskUserQuestion` with abstract framing when concrete code investigation would let the user answer with much less cognitive load.\n   - **User check-in \"Didn't we fix all the retrying???\"**: I explained clearly and without being defensive that each canon-exit-gate retry on Track 9's spec had caught a genuinely different, real defect (not a repeated/unfixed bug), and explicitly offered to reduce rigor if the user preferred; user replied \"Keep going then,\" confirming continued full rigor.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Solved: recovering and correctly persisting already-approved-but-uncommitted work (specs, plans, doc fixes) via careful hunk-level diff splitting, preserving the \"never batch unrelated fixes into one commit\" discipline even when reconstructing after the fact.\n   - Solved: efficiently applying small, precisely-evidenced spec/plan-review findings via direct Python JSON manipulation instead of re-dispatching full drafting/planning rounds — used dozens of times across Track 1, Track 2, and Track 9's spec-gating cycles.\n   - Solved: correctly distinguishing \"genuine spec contradiction requiring canon/correct\" from \"plan narrative was imprecise, spec text itself was fine\" (Track 2's two discovery-limitation incidents) — the former got a full correction cycle, the latter didn't, based on careful reading of the actual criterion text vs. the plan's paraphrase.\n   - Solved: unblocking two genuine product/architecture decisions (Track 3a match semantics, Track 4/6 wire format) by combining direct code investigation with narrowly-scoped user questions, arriving at low-risk, well-evidenced resolutions for both (doc-only fix for 3a; scoped schemaVersion bump for 2/6).\n   - Ongoing: Track 9's implementation has NOT yet started — the spec is gated and committed, but no `vector:planner` dispatch has occurred yet for it. This is the immediate next step, explicitly signaled by my own in-flight text right before the summary request: \"Writing to disk and committing, then dispatching vector-planner.\"\n   - Ongoing/deferred (explicitly, by design, not blocking anything): Track 4 and Track 6-mechanical (release-pipeline/JSON-report-contract, now unblocked by the wire-format decision but not yet spec'd or started); Track 5 (depends on Track 1(b), not yet checked whether now unblocked); Track 7, 8-mechanical, 11/12 (no blockers, not yet reached); Track 8-feature, Track 10 (deliberately deferred per the original master plan, not scheduled).\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - (AskUserQuestion selection) \"Do Track 1 first, then Track 2 (Recommended)\"\n   - \"keep going with expediency but keeping the highest level of quality and keeping our token usage in mind\"\n   - \"What happened??\"\n   - \"Are you stuck??\"\n   - \"Is the exig gate ready?\" [sic, \"exit gate\"]\n   - \"Are we not using the new plugins that should be doing better at this?\" (preceded by local command outputs from `/reload-plugins`)\n   - \"How much of our audit have we addressed?\" (with a pasted artifact URL)\n   - \"We were only 20% done track 2 this morning. Why has that not moved at all????\"\n   - \"Ok, keep going with expediency but keeping the highest level of quality and keeping our token usage in mind\" (restated)\n   - \"How do we unblock it?\"\n   - (AskUserQuestion tool rejected) \"The user wants to clarify these questions... they may have additional information, context or questions for you.\"\n   - \"For #1 we need a simple config and unique naming patterns. I was thinking the name in the config and the publish method are unique enough to match right?\"\n   - \"I need more details on decision 2 to help here. Seems like the GH action should be mostly calling the cli and acting on it right? less parsing?\"\n   - (AskUserQuestion selection) \"Bump schemaVersion for MatrixReport + the plan-publish report; correctness-fix the rest (Recommended)\"\n   - \"What's next? Do it\"\n   - (AskUserQuestion selection, re: Track 9 bug 2 scope) \"Full canon pipeline, same rigor as Track 1 (Recommended)\"\n   - \"Didn't we fix all the retrying???\"\n   - \"Keep going then\"\n   - (mid-turn interruption message, addressed inline per system instructions) \"What happened??\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Dispatch `vector:planner` to produce an implementation plan for `SPEC-TRACK9-CASCADE-REWRITE-KEY-CORRECTNESS.json` (7 AC, ~16-32 call sites to touch: `CascadeInput.identity` field, `Workspace.identity` field, 14 `CascadeInput` test-literal updates, 18 `Workspace` test-literal updates, 2 real `CascadeInput` construction sites, the rewrite-key construction fix itself).\n   - Run `vector:challenger` on that plan, apply any findings, persist plan to `.claude/plans/`, commit.\n   - Implement via `lambda:implementer` task-by-task (strict same-file-sequential discipline), `lambda:reviewer`, mutation testing, `lambda:exit-gate`, `just ci`.\n   - Update `ACTIVE.md` marking Track 9 fully DONE once both sub-bugs (peer-escalation — already done — and rewrite-key — pending) are complete.\n   - After Track 9: no explicit next-track instruction has been given yet; per the master plan's phasing and the \"What's next? Do it\" pattern already established, Track 4/Track 6-mechanical (now unblocked by the wire-format decision) or Track 5 (dependency status on Track 1(b) not yet re-checked) are the natural candidates, but nothing should be started there without either an explicit user go-ahead or restating the \"What's next? Do it\"-style open invitation, consistent with how Track 9 was chosen.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   Immediately before this summary was requested, I had just: (a) fixed AC-005 (Workspace.identity field, correcting the `ws.graph.identity()` compile-error design), AC-007 (observable Bare-PackageId fallback), and the CascadeInput.identity api_surface entry (concrete `.native.insert()` pattern) directly in the spec JSON at `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118/scratchpad/spec-track9-draft.json`; (b) dispatched `canon:exit-gate` retry 1, which found and I fixed two more issues (missing 18 `Workspace` struct-literal sites in `scope`; AC-004's now-contradictory exclusive exception clause); (c) dispatched `canon:exit-gate` retry 2, which returned **PASS** (confidence high, zero blockers, all 18 file:line citations independently re-verified exact); (d) wrote the passed spec to `.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK9-CASCADE-REWRITE-KEY-CORRECTNESS.json` and committed it as `fea63de` with a detailed commit message documenting the full 3-round exit-gate history (compile-error catch, scope-omission catch, final pass). The commit tool call and its successful result (`[main fea63de] docs(specs): add gated spec for Track 9 cascade rewrite-key correctness ... 1 file changed, 71 insertions(+) create mode 100644 .claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK9-CASCADE-REWRITE-KEY-CORRECTNESS.json`) was the last completed action in the conversation before the summary request arrived. No `vector:planner` dispatch had yet occurred for this spec.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Dispatch `vector:planner` (subagent_type: `vector:planner`) to produce a plan@1 for `SPEC-TRACK9-CASCADE-REWRITE-KEY-CORRECTNESS.json`, directly continuing the exact pipeline sequence already used for Track 1 and Track 2 in this same session (canon spec passed → vector-planner → vector-challenger → write plan to `.claude/plans/`, commit → lambda implementation). This is the direct, un-interrupted continuation of my own immediately-prior stated intent: \"Writing to disk and committing, then dispatching vector-planner\" (stated right before the spec-commit tool calls that just completed). No new user instruction is needed to take this step — it is the same established, already-approved (via \"Full canon pipeline, same rigor as Track 1\" and \"Keep going then\") pipeline continuing to its next stage. The plan should account for the api_surface's now-corrected design (real `Workspace.identity: IdentityIndex` field, not `ws.graph.identity()`) and the full enumerated site list (2 real CascadeInput sites, 14 CascadeInput test sites, 18 Workspace test sites) exactly as specified in the now-committed, exit-gate-passed spec.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"0892eba7-4794-4223-a34c-316f2f28503b","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:00:09.548Z","session_id":"bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
