{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-19T16:30:21.608Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>af1db6da68a74765e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01FkP2Xg8JuPG1PWCWDewJh8</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/af1db6da68a74765e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit Track 4 spec\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I have enough grounding. Here is the audit output.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"The api_surface entry for `resolve_commit` documents its signature as `Result&lt;Option&lt;CommitSha&gt;, VcsError&gt;` — it is fallible and can return `Ok(None)` (tag ref does not resolve despite `contains_tag` reporting true — a plausible race or symbolic-ref edge case) or `Err(VcsError)` (git failure during resolution). Every acceptance criterion touching the sha-correction fix (AC-06 through AC-09) only specifies behavior for the happy path where `resolve_commit` successfully returns `Some(sha)`. There is no criterion describing what `create_tags_with_options` must do when `resolve_commit` returns `Ok(None)` or `Err(...)` for an already-existing tag. An implementer following the spec literally could `.unwrap()` or silently fall back to `release.sha` (recreating the exact fabrication bug this spec exists to fix) on that path and still pass every stated AC.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add: \\\"AC-06b: When `callisto tag` processes a release whose tag already exists (`contains_tag` returns true) and `resolve_commit` against that tag's ref returns `Ok(None)` or an `Err(VcsError)`, the command does not fabricate a `sha` value for that `CreatedTag` entry by falling back to `release.sha`. Instead it surfaces this as a diagnostic entry in the command's output (with the affected `tag_name` and a message indicating the tag's target could not be resolved) and either omits the `sha` field's guarantee of correctness by marking the diagnostic severity as Warning while still emitting the entry with the best-effort value it has, or the command exits non-zero. [Domain input needed to resolve which of these two behaviors is correct — see paired 'incomplete' issue below.] Verifiable by running `callisto tag` against a fixture where the tag ref exists per `contains_tag` but is deliberately broken (e.g. a symbolic ref pointing at a missing object) and confirming the JSON output flags the mismatch rather than silently reporting `release.sha`.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"incomplete\",\n      \"description\": \"Directly caused by the gap above: the spec's own non_goals and acceptance_criteria never state whether a `resolve_commit` failure on an already-existing tag should (a) fail the whole `callisto tag` invocation non-zero, (b) succeed but attach a diagnostic while still emitting a best-effort `CreatedTag`, or (c) something else. This is a real design decision — not a wording problem — and cannot be resolved by rewording existing criteria. It requires the spec author to decide whether tag-resolution failure is treated as fatal (consistent with `plan_publish`'s 'never panic or error the whole plan' non-goal for changelog reads) or as a hard error (consistent with 'never fabricate reported state', the spec's own stated purpose).\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Cannot be produced without a domain decision from the spec owner. Recommend adding an explicit non_goal or acceptance criterion stating one of: \\\"A `resolve_commit` failure on an already-existing tag causes `create_tags_with_options` to return `Err(GraphError)` immediately, matching the fail-fast precedent of git access failures elsewhere in this function\\\" OR \\\"A `resolve_commit` failure on an already-existing tag is non-fatal; the entry's `sha` is omitted/marked and a Warning-severity diagnostic is added to `TagReport.diagnostics`, and the command still exits 0.\\\" Pick one before this spec is implementable end-to-end.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-10/AC-11/AC-12 cover exactly two states for changelog reading: file exists with a matching heading (AC-10), file does not exist (AC-11), and file exists but the heading is absent (AC-12). They do not cover a third observable class of failure: the file exists but cannot be read as valid UTF-8, or a read fails for a reason other than 'not found' (permission denied, `pkg_dir` resolving to a path component that collides with a non-regular file, etc.). The api_surface's `extract_section` entry says `plan_publish` must call it 'reading the file only when it exists' and 'must never panic or error the whole plan on either condition [missing file / missing heading]' — but says nothing about a read that fails after the existence check succeeds (TOCTOU) or a non-UTF8 file, which `String`-based reads in Rust will error on (`fs::read_to_string` returns `Err` on invalid UTF-8, not silently). The confirmed live code at publish.rs:290-298 also shows `pkg_dir` can legitimately be an empty `PathBuf` when a package has no manifests, meaning `{pkg_dir}/CHANGELOG.md` resolves to a root-relative `CHANGELOG.md` that may exist but belong to an unrelated package or the workspace root — a case none of AC-10/11/12 anticipate.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add: \\\"AC-12b: When the package's resolved `pkg_dir` is empty (no manifest to derive a directory from), `plan_publish` treats `changelog_section` as `None` without attempting to read any CHANGELOG.md — it must not fall back to reading a workspace-root or unrelated-package CHANGELOG.md. Verifiable by running `callisto plan-publish --format json` against a workspace fixture containing a publishable package with zero manifests and a workspace-root CHANGELOG.md that does contain a matching `## {version}` heading, and confirming that package's `changelogSection` field is `null` (not the root file's content).\\\" and \\\"AC-12c: When `{pkg_dir}/CHANGELOG.md` exists but cannot be read as valid UTF-8 or fails to read for a reason other than not-found, `plan_publish` treats `changelog_section` as `None` for that package, adds a Warning-severity diagnostic naming the package and the read failure, and does not error the whole plan or panic. Verifiable by running `callisto plan-publish --format json` against a fixture where a package's CHANGELOG.md contains invalid UTF-8 bytes, and confirming the command exits 0, `changelogSection` is `null` for that package, and a diagnostic referencing the package appears in the JSON output.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-09\",\n      \"type\": \"ambiguous\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-09 does not state an independently testable requirement — it restates AC-06 with hedge language ('either... or...', 'is sufficient here', 'at minimum'). Its actual binding clause ('AC-06's corrected sha value must never equal the requested release.sha when the two differ') is already logically guaranteed by AC-06 (which requires sha to equal the tag's true target, and the criterion's own premise is that the true target differs from release.sha). As written, AC-09 cannot fail independently of AC-06 — there is no observable behavior that satisfies AC-06 but violates AC-09 or vice versa, which makes it an untestable duplicate rather than a distinct proposition. This wastes an implementer's time trying to find a distinct thing to verify, and its OR-with-an-escape-hatch phrasing ('is sufficient here') is exactly the kind of vague hedging that lets an ambiguous criterion pass audit by sounding rigorous without adding a checkable constraint.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Delete AC-09 and fold its only substantive addition — that a caller must be able to detect staleness using only the JSON report, with no source-code knowledge — directly into AC-06's text: \\\"AC-06: ...the CreatedTag entry pushed for that release reports a sha value equal to the tag's real, current target commit (obtained via a live lookup such as resolve_commit against the tag ref) — not release.sha — such that a caller comparing the reported sha against the release plan's requested sha can detect the mismatch using only the JSON report, without any knowledge of the implementation. Verifiable by running callisto tag against a repository fixture where a tag pkg@1.0.0 already points at commit A, the release plan requests sha B (A != B), and inspecting the sha field of the corresponding entry in the tool's JSON output: it must equal A, not B.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"ambiguous\",\n      \"description\": \"The api_surface entry for `create_tags_with_options` parenthetically cites two source locations as both currently affected by the sha-fabrication bug: 'tag.rs:113-117 and the floating-major push at tag.rs:103-107'. Reading the live code (tag.rs:99-111), the floating-major push unconditionally calls `git.create_floating_major(major_tag, &amp;release.sha, permit)` before pushing its `CreatedTag` — meaning the floating-major ref is always force-moved to `release.sha` on every run, regardless of whether it previously existed at a different commit. Its reported `sha` is therefore never fabricated; it is always the ref's true post-call target, by construction of `create_floating_major`. Lumping this line in with the genuinely buggy release-tag push (tag.rs:113-117) implies to an implementer that the floating-major site also needs an `already_existed`-style staleness check and a `resolve_commit` call, which would be wasted or actively wrong work (comparing a moving alias's old target against release.sha is not a bug to fix — that's the alias's designed behavior).\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Narrow the api_surface description to: \\\"...currently pushes `CreatedTag { sha: release.sha.clone(), .. }` unconditionally for the per-release tag (tag.rs:113-117) -- the fix must make the reported sha reflect the tag's true existing target when already_existed is true, using GitDataSource::resolve_commit(...). Note: the floating-major alias push at tag.rs:103-107 is out of scope for this fix -- it unconditionally force-moves the alias via git.create_floating_major on every run, so its reported release.sha is always genuinely correct and requires no staleness check.\\\" Add this note to non_goals as well: \\\"The floating-major alias tag's CreatedTag.sha (tag.rs:103-107) -- it is always force-moved to release.sha by create_floating_major and is never subject to the staleness bug AC-06 through AC-09 fix for the per-release tag.\\\"\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Scope-overlap check: grepped .claude/specs/ for the four in-scope files (action.yml, commands/tag.rs, commands/publish.rs, commands/status.rs) and callisto-changelog references. Hits in SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001, SPEC-TRACK3B1/3B2, and track-g-matrix-napi-maturin are incidental (different files entirely: identity.rs, walk.rs, resolve.rs, manifest CST editors, napi/maturin build matrix) -- confirmed no genuine overlap with Track 4's scope. That dimension is clean.\\n\\nUntestable/vague-criteria dimension: mostly clean -- AC-01 through AC-13 are almost all falsifiable from observable behavior (script branch taken, exit code, JSON field value) without requiring implementation knowledge. AC-09 is the exception: it's a hedged restatement of AC-06 that adds no independently checkable constraint, which is exactly the kind of plausible-sounding-but-empty language the audit exists to catch, even though it isn't 'shall respond within an acceptable timeframe' style vagueness.\\n\\nMissing-error-case dimension: this is where the spec is weakest. Two real fallibility surfaces are documented in the api_surface (resolve_commit's Result&lt;Option&lt;...&gt;,...&gt; return type; extract_section's file-read precondition) but neither is carried through into acceptance criteria for its failure paths. The tag.rs gap is serious because the whole point of this track is 'stop fabricating state,' and an unaddressed resolve_commit failure path is exactly where fabrication could silently reappear. I verified this against the live create_tags_with_options code (tag.rs:30-125) and confirmed the current code has no such handling to inherit -- this is genuinely unaddressed, not implicitly covered elsewhere. The changelog gap (non-UTF8 read failure, empty-pkg_dir collision) is confirmed against the live pkg_dir resolution code at publish.rs:290-298, which shows pkg_dir can legitimately be empty.\\n\\nAmbiguous-phrasing dimension: one real instance -- the api_surface's citation of tag.rs:103-107 alongside tag.rs:113-117 as both bug sites, when the floating-major push at 103-107 is actually never buggy by construction. Confirmed by reading the code.\\n\\nIncomplete-sections dimension: no TBD placeholders anywhere in the document, but the resolve_commit failure-mode decision is a genuine open design question, not a wording problem -- flagged as incomplete rather than guessed at, per the instructions.\\n\\nOverall verdict is fail because of the missing-error-case findings on resolve_commit fallibility: a spec whose entire purpose is 'stop silently reporting fabricated state' cannot pass audit while leaving its own newly-introduced fallible call's failure path completely unspecified -- that is precisely the kind of gap that reproduces the bug class this track exists to close.\"\n}\n```\n\n**Files consulted (read-only, no writes made):**\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/` (all existing spec files, to confirm no Track 4 overlap)\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml` (lines 1–200, confirmed the exit-code branching bug matches spec's claims)\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs` (full `create_tags_with_options`, confirmed the floating-major push is not actually buggy, and confirmed no existing handling for `resolve_commit` failure)\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs` (lines 260–320, confirmed `pkg_dir` can be empty)\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-changelog/src/write.rs` (`extract_section`, lines 102+)\n\nSummary: no scope overlap with existing specs. The audit fails on two missing-error-case findings tied to the spec's own documented fallible APIs (`resolve_commit`'s `Result&lt;Option&lt;...&gt;&gt;`, and changelog file reads beyond \"missing\"/\"no heading\"), one genuinely ambiguous api_surface citation (the floating-major tag site doesn't actually have the bug it's grouped with), and one redundant/hedged criterion (AC-09) that adds no checkable content beyond AC-06. One of the missing-error-case issues is a true \"incomplete\" (needs a domain decision, not a rewrite) per the output contract's instruction not to guess.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>59823</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>8</tool_uses><duration_ms>137225</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
