{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-19T16:39:47.049Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9957c32483ecb88d</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_016c6HNyMwqk1QGXSk996Sna</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/a9957c32483ecb88d.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Exit-gate Track 4 spec\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I verified every citation against live code and traced the fixes. Verdict below.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"artifact_type\": \"spec@1\",\n  \"retry_count\": 2,\n  \"verdict_summary\": \"FAIL. Citations verified accurate; audit fixes are present. Six blockers: --dry-run path (tag.rs:53-80) unspecified and a non_goals claim about it is false; exit-code-0 silently passes under AC-03/AC-04; AC-11/12/12c leave the DiagnosticCode choice open; AC-10 is false for an empty section body.\",\n  \"blockers\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"AC-06, AC-06b, AC-07, AC-08 (and scope item 3)\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs:53-80; /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/tag.rs:58-65\",\n      \"finding\": \"Every tag criterion is conditioned on `contains_tag` returning true, but `contains_tag` is only evaluated on the apply path (tag.rs:82). When `permit` is None (`callisto tag --dry-run`, permit built at cli/commands/tag.rs:58 via `ApplyPermit::granted_unless_dry_run`), control takes the `let Some(permit) = permit else { ... continue }` branch at tag.rs:53-80, which pushes `CreatedTag { sha: release.sha.clone(), .. }` at tag.rs:74-78 with no existence check at all, and the identical JSON TagReport is written to stdout at cli/commands/tag.rs:63. So the exact fabricated-sha output this spec exists to eliminate remains reachable through a shipped, documented flag, and the spec never says whether that is in scope. Worse, AC-06's verification method says only 'by running `callisto tag`' — a tester who reasonably uses `--dry-run` to avoid mutating the fixture repo would observe B, not A, against a fully correct implementation of the apply path. REQUIRED CHANGE: (a) add an explicit criterion, e.g. AC-06c, stating what `CreatedTag.sha` must contain on the dry-run path (permit == None) for a release whose tag already exists — either 'must also report the tag's real current target via resolve_commit' or 'is out of scope and continues to report release.sha' — and if the latter, also add a matching non_goals entry naming tag.rs:74-78; (b) amend AC-06, AC-07, and AC-08's verification methods to state the invocation is without `--dry-run` (permit granted), so the fixture is deterministic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"non_goals, final entry ('The floating-major alias tag's CreatedTag.sha (tag.rs:103-107)...')\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs:64-69 vs 102-107\",\n      \"finding\": \"This entry — newly added in this revision — asserts the alias sha 'is unconditionally force-moved to release.sha by create_floating_major on every run, so its reported sha is always genuinely correct by construction and is never subject to the staleness bug'. That is false as written. It holds only on the apply path (git.create_floating_major at tag.rs:102, immediately followed by the push at tag.rs:103-107). On the dry-run path, tag.rs:64-69 pushes the alias `CreatedTag` with `sha: release.sha.clone()` and `create_floating_major` is never called, so the alias sha reported for `callisto tag --floating-major --dry-run` is exactly as stale as the per-release bug this spec fixes. A drafter or planner relying on this non_goal will skip a live defect on the strength of a claim the code does not support. REQUIRED CHANGE: restrict the claim to the apply path — 'on every apply run (permit granted, tag.rs:102)' — and state explicitly what the dry-run alias push at tag.rs:64-69 must report, consistent with whatever AC-06c decides for the per-release dry-run push.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"AC-03 and AC-04\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml:90-97 (else-branch `exit $STATUS_EXIT` at line 96)\",\n      \"finding\": \"AC-03 states only a negative ('the script's branching logic must not contain a code path that maps exit-code 0 to HAS_CHANGESETS=true') and never says what the script must do on exit 0. AC-04 covers 'any code other than 1 or 2', which includes 0, and requires termination 'with a non-zero exit' — but its verification method only stubs 127. The minimal fix that satisfies both as written is to delete the `-eq 0` arm and let 0 fall into the existing else-branch, which ends in `exit $STATUS_EXIT` (line 96): on exit 0 that is `exit 0`. The step then prints an ::error:: annotation and succeeds. Since there is no `set -u` (line 80 is `set -e` only), `HAS_CHANGESETS` is unset, `[[ \\\"$HAS_CHANGESETS\\\" == \\\"true\\\" ]]` at line 100 is false, and the action proceeds into the publish branch — a silent publish on an unrecognised exit code, precisely the failure class this spec is written to close. An implementer must make a judgment call about what 'correct' means here. REQUIRED CHANGE: rewrite AC-03 to state the positive requirement (exit code 0 is not a value `--check` ever returns; it must take the unexpected-exit-code branch, emit the ::error:: annotation, and terminate the step with a non-zero exit code), and extend AC-04's verification method to include an exit-0 stub asserting the step fails non-zero — which forces the else-branch to stop using bare `exit $STATUS_EXIT`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"AC-12c (interacting with AC-13 and non_goals entry 2)\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/diagnostic.rs:98-170\",\n      \"finding\": \"AC-12c requires 'a Warning-severity diagnostic naming the package and the read failure' but does not name the DiagnosticCode. No existing variant fits: `ChangelogSectionNotFound` (diagnostic.rs:142-144) is documented as 'could not locate a release's section', which is the not-found case, not an IO/UTF-8 read failure; `ChangesetReadError` (diagnostic.rs:146-148) is about changesets, not changelogs. The implementer must therefore either reuse a semantically wrong code or add a new `DiagnosticCode` variant — and AC-13 requires that no field of these report types be 'added, removed, renamed, or retyped', verified by diffing `callisto schema` output, where a new variant changes the serialized enum domain of the `code` field. AC-12c and AC-13 are therefore in tension and neither resolves it. REQUIRED CHANGE: name the exact code AC-12c must emit (either an existing variant, or a specific new one such as `ChangelogReadError` spelled out verbatim), and amend AC-13 to state explicitly whether adding a `DiagnosticCode` enum variant is permitted — if it is, scope AC-13's no-change claim to struct fields and exempt the `code` field's enum value set; if it is not, AC-12c must reuse a named existing variant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"AC-11 and AC-12\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/diagnostic.rs:142-144\",\n      \"finding\": \"AC-11 (no CHANGELOG.md) and AC-12 (no matching `## {version}` heading) specify only that `changelog_section` stays None and that the command does not error or panic, and are silent on whether a diagnostic is emitted. `DiagnosticCode::ChangelogSectionNotFound` already exists and its doc comment reads 'Reserved for `plan-publish` reporting that it could not locate a release's section in its package's generated CHANGELOG.md (v0.2); not emitted by any current code path' — this spec is exactly the code path that would activate that reserved variant. Two implementations, one emitting it and one not, both satisfy AC-11 and AC-12 as written while producing different `diagnostics` arrays in the JSON output that consumers can observe. That is a judgment call about what 'correct' means. REQUIRED CHANGE: state in AC-11 and AC-12 whether a diagnostic must be emitted on those paths; if yes, name the code (`ChangelogSectionNotFound`) and severity, and add to each verification method an assertion on the presence or absence of that diagnostic in the JSON output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion\": \"AC-10\",\n      \"location\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-changelog/src/write.rs:131-136\",\n      \"finding\": \"AC-10 asserts `changelog_section` is `Some(section_text)` whenever CHANGELOG.md 'exists and contains a `## {version}` heading matching the release's version'. That universal is false against the real `extract_section`: after slicing, it does `let raw_section = slice_after[..end].trim(); if raw_section.is_empty() { None } else { Some(raw_section) }` (write.rs:131-136), so a matching heading with an empty or whitespace-only body returns `None`. As written, AC-10 is a falsifiable proposition that the correct implementation falsifies. REQUIRED CHANGE: reword AC-10's condition to '...contains a `## {version}` heading matching the release's version AND that section has non-empty content after trimming, where section_text is what extract_section returns', and add the empty-section case explicitly — either as a new is_error_case criterion asserting `changelogSection` is null when the matching heading exists but its body is empty, or as an added clause on AC-12.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"Citation audit — all verified against live code, all accurate: action.yml step 'Inspecting Workspace Status' and its if/elif/else chain (lines 85-97, bug confirmed: exit 0 maps to HAS_CHANGESETS=true, exit 1 falls into the error branch); main.rs doc comment lines 19-21 verbatim ('0 when no errors and (no --check OR at least one pending changeset)') — AC-05's citation and its word-for-word comparison target are both exact; status.rs:18 check_exit_code_raw, body through :28, handle's --check arm at :53-54; tag.rs:30 create_tags_with_options signature exact, :48 git_access, :82 contains_tag, :103-107 floating-major push, :113-117 per-release push; write.rs:102 extract_section signature exact; publish.rs:290-298 pkg_dir resolution exact (ReleaseEntry with hardcoded changelog_section: None is at publish.rs:427). The only other `changelog_section: None` in the tree (cli/src/commands/publish.rs:361) is inside a #[cfg(test)] module — no sibling gap there. Audit-fix verification: AC-06b's fail-fast decision is implementable (GraphError::Vcs is #[from] VcsError, error.rs:59; CLI maps Err to exit 1); the api_surface correction about tag.rs:103-107 is right about the apply path but wrong about dry-run (blocker 2); AC-12b matches the real unwrap_or_default at publish.rs:295-298. Minor advisories, not blockers: (a) the spec labels resolve_commit as `GitDataSource::resolve_commit` at lib.rs:291, but that line is GitRepository's inherent method — access.rs:79 is the trait impl, so the pair of citations is fine, just mislabeled; (b) the spec never says which ref string to pass to resolve_commit (short `pkg@1.0.0` vs `refs/tags/pkg@1.0.0`), which matters because GitAccess falls back to ShellGit (access.rs:79-86) and a same-named branch would resolve differently — AC-06's fixture pins the observable outcome so this is survivable; (c) pkg_dir is workspace-root-relative per the comment at publish.rs:287-289 and the spec's `{pkg_dir}/CHANGELOG.md` shorthand does not say to join against the workspace root. Also noted: plan-publish has no --strict flag (cli.rs:181-185), so the AC-12c warning cannot flip an exit code; TagIndex is a cached OnceCell snapshot (lib.rs:152-168, tags.rs:164) so contains_tag/resolve_commit can legitimately disagree, which is exactly AC-06b's fixture.\"\n}\n```\n\nHeadline: this revision fixed what the first audit found, and every file:line citation in the spec checks out against live code — I verified all of them myself rather than trusting the spec. It still fails, on six items, two of which are new.\n\nThe dominant one is the `--dry-run` path. All four tag criteria hinge on `contains_tag`, which is only reached when a permit exists (`/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs:82`). With `--dry-run` the function takes the else-branch at tag.rs:53-80 and pushes `sha: release.sha.clone()` with no existence check, and `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/tag.rs:63` writes the same JSON report. The fabricated sha survives the fix through a shipped flag, and AC-06's verification (\"by running `callisto tag`\") doesn't say which mode to run in — so a tester using `--dry-run` on a fixture repo would fail a correct implementation.\n\nThat same gap makes the newly-added final `non_goals` entry factually wrong: the floating-major alias is *not* \"unconditionally force-moved on every run\" — on the dry-run path tag.rs:64-69 pushes the alias sha while `create_floating_major` is never called. That entry currently instructs a downstream planner to skip a live defect.\n\nThe other new-issue find worth calling out is the exit-code-0 hole: the minimal fix satisfying AC-03 and AC-04 as written lets 0 fall into the else-branch ending in `exit $STATUS_EXIT` (`/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml:96`), which is `exit 0`. With no `set -u`, `HAS_CHANGESETS` is unset, line 100 evaluates false, and the action publishes — silently, on an unrecognised exit code.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>73711</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>318516</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
