nch is never taken. Case (2)'s rationale was also corrected to name both real gates (cascade.rs:501's `sev > cur_sev` gate for the cascade to fire at all, AND the same-base-version constraint for the reason to not be overwritten back). The `target > cur_sev` gate-identifier typo (should be `sev > cur_sev`) was corrected in both AC-004 and AC-005 wherever it appeared.\n\nVerify all of this against live code with real rigor -- this is genuinely the last round before the retry_count exceeds 3 and escalation becomes mandatory per your own protocol, so do not wave anything through. Specifically:\n- Re-derive the full {changelog_inputs, reason} state-coverage table one more time and confirm AC-003 no longer double-claims exclusivity against AC-004 on the {None, Inference, is-a-fixed-group-member} state (confirm the fixture exclusion actually closes this, not just relocates it).\n- Actually trace cascade.rs:400-446 and groups.rs:97-142 yourself for the \"both members share the same starting manifest base version\" scenario and confirm fixed_group_target's winner really does equal bump_target(fresh, sev)'s own result in that specific configuration, so cascade.rs:433-440's overwrite branch is genuinely never taken -- construct the trace concretely (two members, both at 1.0.0, one tagged/released, one fresh; fresh gets a changeset-driven Patch bump; separately for case 2, fresh also gets a Cascade-driven Minor bump exceeding the changeset's severity) and confirm both of AC-004's asserted outcomes hold.\n- Re-verify every other criterion (AC-001, AC-002, AC-005, AC-006, AC-007) fresh against live code one more time, not on the strength of three prior passing verifications -- code may have drifted, and this session has precedent for exactly that.\n\nIf this passes, it moves to vector:planner next -- state that explicitly. If it fails, state plainly that retry_count now exceeds the threshold and this must go to human escalation, with no further automated rounds suggested."},"cal