orm-manifest version-write and optional-dependency-update logic iterates only `ws.config.groups.fixed.values()` — no equivalent pass over `ws.config.groups.linked`. Likely intentional (platform/native-addon siblings are architecturally always fixed-group members), but nothing in the code or a doc comment states that invariant explicitly at this call site, so the omission of a `linked` pass reads as an implicit special case rather than a documented contract.\nFAILURE_SCENARIO: If linked groups are ever extended to carry `GroupMember::PlatformManifest` members (nothing in `GroupMember`'s type prevents this), those platform manifests would silently never receive version writes or optional-dep updates here, and the bug would only surface as \"my linked-group platform package didn't get bumped\" rather than a compile-time or validation-time error — because the loop scope was narrowed by omission, not by an enforced type-level constraint. Low confidence this is a real bug rather than settled architecture; flagging because the boundary isn't documented.\n\nNo other genuine altitude-bandaid candidates found in my assigned scope (commands/version.rs, config/groups.rs, infer.rs, tags.rs, plan.rs, config/raw.rs).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>158451</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>180999</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:22:52.953Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab7e93debcdc8258f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_013YE2Y6ArB1krbRKeoCtK8F</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ab7e93debcdc8258f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Angle G: altitude / architectural fit\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Investigation complete. I reviewed the diff systematically (matrix.rs, resolve.rs, identity.rs, membership.rs/ignore_walk.rs, groups.rs, walk.rs, error/diagnostic files, registry.rs) directly, plus dispatched a fork to cover version.rs/tags.rs/infer.rs/plan.rs/raw.rs. Note: matrix.rs, tags.rs, infer.rs, ecosystem.rs, and path.rs turned out to be genuine generalizing refactors (shared parse-once helpers, shared GitAccess handle, Option&lt;NpmAccess&gt; instead of a lossy bool) — not bandaids, so they're excluded below.\n\nFindings (JSON array):\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs\",\n    \"line\": 99,\n    \"summary\": \"IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() has three near-identical copy-pasted blocks for discovering Cargo/npm/Python packages (lines 72-97, 99-118, 120-146). The Cargo and npm blocks each consult a workspace-membership check (CargoMembership::admits at membership.rs:34-60, NpmMembership::admits at membership.rs:222-245) built by two independently hand-written structs with duplicated admits()/hybrid_root logic and no shared trait. The Python block (line 120-146) has NO membership check at all -- there is no PythonMembership type anywhere in membership.rs.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Any Python monorepo using a workspace-membership mechanism (e.g. uv's [tool.uv.workspace] members/exclude, or a poetry monorepo with path deps and an exclude list) gets zero filtering: every pyproject.toml under the tree is admitted as a package regardless of what a real Python workspace config says, while the equivalent Cargo/npm case is correctly filtered. Adding that support later requires hand-rolling a third parallel MembershipXxx struct and manually splicing a third copy-pasted if-block into projects(), instead of extending one shared 'ecosystem membership' abstraction -- exactly the kind of per-ecosystem bandaid the walker's own design already committed to for Cargo and npm.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs\",\n    \"line\": 184,\n    \"summary\": \"The per-manifest (format, filename) lookup used while building ManifestDecls is a match on Ecosystem with an explicit `_ =&gt; (ManifestFormat::PackageJson, \\\"package.json\\\")` catch-all for any ecosystem that isn't Cargo/Npm/Pypi.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The next Ecosystem variant added (the codebase already has RegistryClient/PublishTarget stubs for NuGet and GitHub Release, so a NuGet C#/.csproj ecosystem is plausible) will silently be treated as if it declares a package.json/PackageJson manifest -- producing a wrong ManifestDecl that is never surfaced as an error, rather than failing loudly or being routed through a per-ecosystem table the way Cargo/Npm/Pypi are. A generalized fix would make this match exhaustive (no wildcard) so the compiler forces every new Ecosystem variant to supply its own filename/format here.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs\",\n    \"line\": 229,\n    \"summary\": \"The bare-name-to-Prefixed-id 'promotion' logic that runs when two packages in different ecosystems share a directory/name is implemented as five separately-numbered, hand-synchronized point-fixes labelled in comments as 'STALE-KEY REWRITE location (1)' through '(5)' (lines 248, 268, 271, 284) scattered through one long function, each re-inserting into a different index map (index.bare, index.native, index.prefixed, package_manifest_decls, promoted_siblings) to undo the staleness the promotion itself introduces.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The comments themselves admit this replaced 'exactly the AC-04 manifests-bleed bug' -- i.e. this is a pile of individually-discovered stale-key patches bolted onto the same mutable-map-rebuild function rather than a single coherent 're-key everything derived from this identity' mechanism (e.g. a promote_identity() helper that atomically rewrites all five structures from one list). Any future map added to this index (there are already five) requires a sixth manually-numbered location to be found and patched by inspection, with no compiler or type-level guarantee that all of them stay in sync -- the same class of bug (AC-04) can recur under a new field with nothing forcing a fix.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs\",\n    \"line\": 149,\n    \"summary\": \"The new cross-group membership-conflict check (claimed_by BTreeMap lookup + ConflictingGroupMembership error, ~9 lines) is duplicated verbatim between the `for rg in &amp;raw.fixed` loop (lines 149-157) and the `for rg in &amp;raw.linked` loop (lines 190-198), instead of being factored into one shared helper called from both.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A future change to the conflict rule (e.g. treating fixed-vs-linked overlap differently from linked-vs-linked overlap) must be applied in two separate physical locations; the two copies already diverge slightly in this same diff (`index.platform.get(name)` destructuring changed independently in both), so an editor patching only one copy would silently leave the other loop's conflicts unchecked or checked with different logic.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs\",\n    \"line\": 446,\n    \"summary\": \"IdentityIndex::resolve_native (line ~415) treats a single cross-ecosystem candidate as resolvable (`1 =&gt; Some(candidates[0])`), but the newly touched resolve_native_with_fallback (line 446, match at line 466) was changed to fold the single-candidate case into the same branch as true (&gt;1) ambiguity -- it now emits an 'is ambiguous across ecosystems' diagnostic and returns None even when there is exactly one candidate. The doc comment ('Like resolve_native but pushes a Diagnostic when cross-ecosystem ambiguity is detected') was not updated and no longer describes the actual divergence.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The only current caller (walk.rs:540) got the behavior it needed for one scenario, but the two 'resolve_native*' functions -- which read as a matched pair differing only by diagnostic-emission -- now silently disagree on whether a single cross-ecosystem match is resolvable at all. A future caller reading the doc comment and reaching for resolve_native_with_fallback expecting resolve_native's contract 'plus diagnostics' will get single-candidate lookups incorrectly rejected as ambiguous (the diagnostic message itself would read '...is ambiguous across ecosystems: cargo:serde' with only one name listed, which is self-contradictory).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs\",\n    \"line\": 141,\n    \"summary\": \"known_credential_env_values() redacts subprocess-stderr credentials by matching a fixed, hardcoded set of env var names (NPM_TOKEN, TWINE_PASSWORD, CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN, GITHUB_TOKEN, GH_TOKEN, plus a CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN pattern) rather than a generalized 'this looks like a secret' heuristic (e.g. any TOKEN/SECRET/PASSWORD/KEY-suffixed env var) or a mechanism where each RegistryClient impl declares its own credential var names.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"callisto's own config already supports publish-to = [\\\"nuget\\\", \\\"github-release\\\"] (resolve.rs) and PublishTargetNotImplemented exists precisely because those dispatchers aren't wired up yet -- but there is no NUGET_API_KEY (or equivalent) entry anywhere in this list already. The moment NuGet publishing is implemented, its credential will leak into redacted stderr/JSON diagnostics unless someone remembers to hand-add its env var name to this one function; the same gap will recur for every future registry target because the mechanism is a manually maintained enumeration, not a generalized detector.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nKey files referenced: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/membership.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs`.\n\nAreas checked and found clean (genuine generalizations, not bandaids): `matrix.rs` (parse-once refactor shared across napi/maturin), `tags.rs`/`infer.rs` (shared GitAccess handle instead of per-call rediscovery), `ecosystem.rs` (bool→Option&lt;NpmAccess&gt; fixing a real information-loss bug generally), `path.rs` (platform-native separator fix via path_slash). A fork subagent additionally reviewed `commands/version.rs`, `tags.rs`, `infer.rs`, `plan.rs`, `config/raw.rs` and surfaced one low-confidence, unverified candidate (version.rs's platform-write loop iterating only `groups.fixed`, never `groups.linked`) — flagged by the fork as possibly intentional architecture rather than a bug, so I left it out of the top 6 in favor of stronger, verified findings.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>160768</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>54</tool_uses><duration_ms>373080</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
