{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:24:17.904Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab811faabab9fdb7a</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ab811faabab9fdb7a.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Angle B: removed-behavior auditor\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All five parallel audit forks have returned. I cross-verified the two candidates they disagreed on or flagged, and refuted one (probe_git deletion — confirmed via `git grep` that it was already dead/unreachable code on `origin/main`, so removing it introduces no behavior change despite `docs/01-spec.md` still describing it as active). Investigation complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAudited the full `origin/main...HEAD` diff (~28K lines) across `crates/`, `docs/`, and `deny.toml` using 5 parallel sub-audits (locate/, walk+matrix, config+identity, manifests, model+vcs), then independently verified every candidate finding by reading current code and running targeted greps. Most of the diff is a well-tested, deliberate refactor (mutate/persist split in manifests, cross-ecosystem identity promotion, parse-once caching in matrix.rs, credential redaction in shell.rs) with re-established invariants at every deletion site I traced. Two genuine findings survived scrutiny; everything else (including one initially-plausible finding — the deleted `probe_git` git-version gate — which I disproved by confirming it had zero callers even on `origin/main`) was refuted with concrete evidence.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/deny.toml\",\n    \"line\": 55,\n    \"summary\": \"Three new RUSTSEC unmaintained-crate advisories (RUSTSEC-2026-0250 im-rc, -0251 sized-chunks, -0247 bitmaps, all transitive via wasm-compose -&gt; wasmtime) were added to the ignore list, each with only a one-line inline comment as justification. No corresponding upgrade, vendoring, or removal of the wasmtime-chain dependency accompanies the change.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The ignore list has no expiry or scheduled re-review mechanism. If any of these transitive crates later receives a real (non-unmaintained) vulnerability advisory, cargo-deny CI will keep passing silently unless someone manually notices and re-audits the ignore list — a standing gap rather than a point-in-time judgment call. (Note: justification is visible and consistent with the commit message 'ignore new unmaintained wasmtime-chain advisories', so this is a lower-confidence/process-hygiene finding rather than a clear regression.)\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs\",\n    \"line\": 417,\n    \"summary\": \"IdentityIndex::resolve_native (line 417-441) retains the old permissive contract — a single cross-ecosystem name match is silently returned as if unambiguous. Its sibling resolve_native_with_fallback (line 446-491) was tightened in this PR to never silently return a cross-ecosystem match, only push a Diagnostic and return None. The two functions now have divergent contracts for the identical input shape.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"resolve_native has zero production call sites today (verified via grep — only its own unit test calls it), so no live path is affected now. But the function remains public API on IdentityIndex; any future caller who reaches for it instead of resolve_native_with_fallback (e.g. because it doesn't require a &amp;mut Vec&lt;Diagnostic&gt;) will silently reintroduce the exact cross-ecosystem misresolution this PR closes elsewhere: a cargo package and an npm package sharing a bare name would have a dependency on 'foo' silently resolved to the wrong ecosystem's package, with zero diagnostic. Low severity since it's dead code today and the divergence is explicitly documented/tested (test name: resolve_native_unchanged_still_returns_cross_ecosystem_match), but it's a latent footgun left in the API surface.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**Refuted (not included above):** `crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/git.rs`'s `probe_git` (git-version compatibility gate) and its `mod.rs` re-export were deleted, and `check_git_version`/`CommandError::IncompatibleVersion` in `callisto-model/src/exec.rs` now have zero callers anywhere in `crates/`. Two of the five forks initially flagged this as a regression, citing `docs/01-spec.md` §G.2.4 which still documents it as mandatory. However, `git grep -n \"probe_git(\" origin/main` returns zero call sites — the function was already unreachable dead code before this PR (only defined and re-exported, never invoked). Its removal changes no runtime behavior; the spec-vs-implementation drift is pre-existing, not introduced by this PR.\n\nEverything else checked out clean across `locate/ignore_walk.rs`/`membership.rs` (workspace-membership filtering fails open on malformed input, not a containment regression; `canonicalize` → `dunce::canonicalize` is a benign Windows-path fix), `walk.rs`/`matrix.rs` (duplicate-package guard narrowing is the PR's stated identity-promotion feature, covered by dedicated tests), `config/resolve.rs` (new `Result` return for `resolve_package_config` is propagated via `?` at both call sites, not swallowed), `callisto-manifests` (persist-on-mutate → explicit `.persist()` split applied consistently at all call sites), and `callisto-vcs/shell.rs` (redaction/arg-injection hardening is additive).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>86443</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>485357</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
