silently resolved to the wrong ecosystem's package, with zero diagnostic. Low severity since it's dead code today and the divergence is explicitly documented/tested (test name: resolve_native_unchanged_still_returns_cross_ecosystem_match), but it's a latent footgun left in the API surface.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**Refuted (not included above):** `crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/git.rs`'s `probe_git` (git-version compatibility gate) and its `mod.rs` re-export were deleted, and `check_git_version`/`CommandError::IncompatibleVersion` in `callisto-model/src/exec.rs` now have zero callers anywhere in `crates/`. Two of the five forks initially flagged this as a regression, citing `docs/01-spec.md` §G.2.4 which still documents it as mandatory. However, `git grep -n \"probe_git(\" origin/main` returns zero call sites — the function was already unreachable dead code before this PR (only defined and re-exported, never invoked). Its removal changes no runtime behavior; the spec-vs-implementation drift is pre-existing, not introduced by this PR.\n\nEverything else checked out clean across `locate/ignore_walk.rs`/`membership.rs` (workspace-membership filtering fails open on malformed input, not a containment regression; `canonicalize` → `dunce::canonicalize` is a benign Windows-path fix), `walk.rs`/`matrix.rs` (duplicate-package guard narrowing is the PR's stated identity-promotion feature, covered by dedicated tests), `config/resolve.rs` (new `Result` return for `resolve_package_config` is propagated via `?` at both call sites, not swallowed), `callisto-manifests` (persist-on-mutate → explicit `.persist()` split applied consistently at all call sites), and `callisto-vcs/shell.rs` (redaction/arg-injection hardening is additive).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>86443</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>485357</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:25:31.577Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab458e5ed15f5a1ad</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01QBche6JJ11ybVVjgNgMbto</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ab458e5ed15f5a1ad.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Angle A3: line-by-line scan of manifests/model/vcs/cli\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Findings\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs\",\n    \"line\": 169,\n    \"summary\": \"In run_with_timeout_impl's stderr reader thread, the pending_line: Vec&lt;u8&gt; buffer used only for the live per-line eprintln echo has no size cap, even though the surrounding code was explicitly rewritten (replacing BufReader::lines()) to bound memory via MAX_CAPTURED_OUTPUT_BYTES/`captured`/`truncated`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A publish subprocess (cargo/npm/twine, or a script it invokes) writes many megabytes to stderr with no newline character at all before exiting or being killed at the wall-clock timeout. `captured` (the value actually returned in CommandOutput.stderr) is correctly capped at MAX_CAPTURED_OUTPUT_BYTES (10MiB), but `pending_line` keeps growing with every byte read since it's only cleared on `\\\\n`, so it accumulates the entire flood in memory uncapped -- exactly the 'buffer unboundedly inside it before ever yielding a line to cap' failure mode the function's own doc comment (lines 160-163) says this custom reader was written to avoid. The two new tests (`run_with_timeout_caps_accumulated_stdout_and_still_drains_the_pipe` / stderr no-newlines variant) only assert on `out.stderr.len()` and elapsed wall-clock time, not on the reader thread's peak internal memory, so they pass despite this gap.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs\",\n    \"line\": 97,\n    \"summary\": \"ShellGit::list_tags runs `git tag --list` and immediately maps stdout lines to tags without ever checking output.success() -- a failing `git tag --list` invocation (rare but real, e.g. a transiently locked/corrupted repo) is silently treated as 'zero tags exist' (Ok(vec![])) rather than surfaced as an Err, unlike every other GitDataSource method in the same impl block (head_sha, commits_since, create_tag, create_floating_major all check output.success() and now redact+propagate stderr).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"On the WASM/CommandRunner fallback path (no gix available), a transient `git tag --list` failure inside a moon extension invocation returns an empty tag list instead of an error. Downstream tag-matching/version-plan logic that treats 'no matching tag found' as 'package has never been released' would then compute a fresh/incorrect baseline version or release-trigger classification for every package in the workspace for that run, instead of the git-discovery failure being surfaced (as `GitDiscoveryFailed`/similar, the pattern the rest of this same PR uses elsewhere for exactly this class of failure).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs\",\n    \"line\": 316,\n    \"summary\": \"CargoToml::update_dependency_spec's workspace-inherited-dependency branch delegates to a freshly-loaded WorkspaceCargoResolver and calls its write_dependency, which persists the ROOT Cargo.toml immediately and returns early -- self.document (the member's own, unmutated document) is never touched. Since the trait's persist-batching contract (this same PR's Spec A/B split) has every caller unconditionally call handle.persist(permit) after update_dependency_spec succeeds (see callisto-graph/src/apply.rs:227-233), that call re-writes the member manifest to disk unchanged whenever this delegation branch is taken.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"If this delegation branch is ever reached through the real apply_version_plan pipeline (defense-in-depth code, not proven unreachable), the member's manifest gets an extra, functionally-inert atomic_write and increments callisto_manifests::persist_call_count() -- the exact counter this PR introduced specifically so tests can assert a manifest is 'flushed to disk at most once per logical operation' (lib.rs:128-134). A redundant-persist regression test built on that counter would not catch this path, and it's a real (if harmless-content) wasted disk write / mtime churn on every affected member file.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nFiles read in full to verify enclosing-function context (all under `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto`):\n- `crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-manifests/src/lib.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs` (not in the requested list, but the sole caller of the manifest mutate/persist split, needed to confirm findings 1 and 3)\n- `crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/status.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs`, `exec.rs`, `identity.rs`, `path.rs`, `report.rs`, `ecosystem.rs`, `plan.rs` (diff-reviewed, no additional high-confidence findings beyond the three above)\n\nAlso checked FINDINGS.md first: none of these three are already logged there, so none are being re-reported.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>238110</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>59</tool_uses><duration_ms>569285</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:25:32.753Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab458e5ed15f5a1ad</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01QBche6JJ11ybVVjgNgMbto</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ab458e5ed15f5a1ad.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Angle A3: line-by-line scan of manifests/model/vcs/cli\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Findings\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs\",\n    \"line\": 169,\n    \"summary\": \"In run_with_timeout_impl's stderr reader thread, the pending_line: Vec&lt;u8&gt; buffer used only for the live per-line eprintln echo has no size cap, even though the surrounding code was explicitly rewritten (replacing BufReader::lines()) to bound memory via MAX_CAPTURED_OUTPUT_BYTES/`captured`/`truncated`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A publish subprocess (cargo/npm/twine, or a script it invokes) writes many megabytes to stderr with no newline character at all before exiting or being killed at the wall-clock timeout. `captured` (the value actually returned in CommandOutput.stderr) is correctly capped at MAX_CAPTURED_OUTPUT_BYTES (10MiB), but `pending_line` keeps growing with every byte read since it's only cleared on `\\\\n`, so it accumulates the entire flood in memory uncapped -- exactly the 'buffer unboundedly inside it before ever yielding a line to cap' failure mode the function's own doc comment (lines 160-163) says this custom reader was written to avoid. The two new tests (`run_with_timeout_caps_accumulated_stdout_and_still_drains_the_pipe` / stderr no-newlines variant) only assert on `out.stderr.len()` and elapsed wall-clock time, not on the reader thread's peak internal memory, so they pass despite this gap.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs\",\n    \"line\": 97,\n    \"summary\": \"ShellGit::list_tags runs `git tag --list` and immediately maps stdout lines to tags without ever checking output.success() -- a failing `git tag --list` invocation (rare but real, e.g. a transiently locked/corrupted repo) is silently treated as 'zero tags exist' (Ok(vec![])) rather than surfaced as an Err, unlike every other GitDataSource method in the same impl block (head_sha, commits_since, create_tag, create_floating_major all check output.success() and now redact+propagate stderr).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"On the WASM/CommandRunner fallback path (no gix available), a transient `git tag --list` failure inside a moon extension invocation returns an empty tag list instead of an error. Downstream tag-matching/version-plan logic that treats 'no matching tag found' as 'package has never been released' would then compute a fresh/incorrect baseline version or release-trigger classification for every package in the workspace for that run, instead of the git-discovery failure being surfaced (as `GitDiscoveryFailed`/similar, the pattern the rest of this same PR uses elsewhere for exactly this class of failure).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs\",\n    \"line\": 316,\n    \"summary\": \"CargoToml::update_dependency_spec's workspace-inherited-dependency branch delegates to a freshly-loaded WorkspaceCargoResolver and calls its write_dependency, which persists the ROOT Cargo.toml immediately and returns early -- self.document (the member's own, unmutated document) is never touched. Since the trait's persist-batching contract (this same PR's Spec A/B split) has every caller unconditionally call handle.persist(permit) after update_dependency_spec succeeds (see callisto-graph/src/apply.rs:227-233), that call re-writes the member manifest to disk unchanged whenever this delegation branch is taken.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"If this delegation branch is ever reached through the real apply_version_plan pipeline (defense-in-depth code, not proven unreachable), the member's manifest gets an extra, functionally-inert atomic_write and increments callisto_manifests::persist_call_count() -- the exact counter this PR introduced specifically so tests can assert a manifest is 'flushed to disk at most once per logical operation' (lib.rs:128-134). A redundant-persist regression test built on that counter would not catch this path, and it's a real (if harmless-content) wasted disk write / mtime churn on every affected member file.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nFiles read in full to verify enclosing-function context (all under `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto`):\n- `crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-manifests/src/lib.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs` (not in the requested list, but the sole caller of the manifest mutate/persist split, needed to confirm findings 1 and 3)\n- `crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/status.rs`\n- `crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs`, `exec.rs`, `identity.rs`, `path.rs`, `report.rs`, `ecosystem.rs`, `plan.rs` (diff-reviewed, no additional high-confidence findings beyond the three above)\n\nAlso checked FINDINGS.md first: none of these three are already logged there, so none are being re-reported.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>238110</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>59</tool_uses><duration_ms>569285</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:29:09.626Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6747ff556a8f5780</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/a6747ff556a8f5780.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify batch 2: symlink/python-membership/list_tags bugs\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Verification Results\n\n### CANDIDATE 1 — `ignore_walk.rs` symlink-as-directory in `projects()`\n\n**Verdict: CONFIRMED**\n\nI read the full `projects()` function (`crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs:39-152`). The `WalkBuilder` is constructed at lines 43-59 with `.hidden(true)`, `.git_ignore(true)`, `.parents(false)`, `.max_depth(Some(32))`, and a `.filter_entry(...)` closure that only excludes entries by name (`target`, `node_modules`, `.git`, `.moon`, `dist`) — there is no `.follow_links(...)` call anywhere in this file or anywhere else in the crate (`grep -n \"follow_links\"` across `crates/callisto-graph/src/` returned nothing), so it uses the `ignore` crate's default of `false`. There is also no `entry.file_type()`/`is_symlink()` check anywhere in the file (`grep -n \"is_symlink|file_type|symlink\"` returned no matches). At line 68, `if !path.is_dir() { continue; }` uses `std::path::Path::is_dir()`, which internally calls `stat` (follows symlinks), not `lstat`. Since `follow_links` is `false`, the walker itself won't recurse *through* a symlinked directory, but it still yields the symlink `DirEntry` once as a leaf; `path.is_dir()` on that leaf returns `true` for a symlink pointing at an external directory, so the code proceeds to `path.join(\"Cargo.toml\")`/`package.json`/`pyproject.toml` and reads the manifest through the symlink, registering it as a `ProjectRoot` with a workspace-relative path computed via `strip_prefix` (line 161) that only strips the *symlink's own* location, not its target. No guard exists to reject this before the `is_dir()` check.\n\n### CANDIDATE 2 — pyproject.toml branch missing membership check\n\n**Verdict: CONFIRMED**\n\nThe full `projects()` body confirms this asymmetry directly. The Cargo branch (lines 72-97) computes `let rel = to_workspace_relative(...)` then gates inclusion with `if cargo_membership.admits(&amp;rel, is_root) { ... }` (line 84). The Npm branch (lines 99-118) does the same with `npm_membership.admits(&amp;rel, is_root)` (line 106). The pyproject.toml branch (lines 120-146) has no equivalent: it parses the TOML, extracts `name` from `[project]` or `[tool.poetry]`, computes `rel`, and unconditionally does `results.push(ProjectRoot { ... })` (lines 138-142) — no `admits`/membership call of any kind. `membership.rs` (`crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/membership.rs`) only exports `CargoMembership` (line 34) and `NpmMembership` (line 222); grep for `Membership` in that file shows no `PythonMembership`/`PyMembership` type exists at all. So every reachable `pyproject.toml` with a resolvable name is included regardless of any Python workspace configuration.\n\n### CANDIDATE 3 — `ShellGit::list_tags` missing success-check\n\n**Verdict: CONFIRMED**\n\nI read the entire `impl GitDataSource for ShellGit&lt;'_&gt;` block (`crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs:75-227`). `list_tags` (lines 97-107) runs `self.runner.run(\"git\", &amp;[\"tag\", \"--list\"], &amp;self.root)?` and immediately does `let all = output.stdout_lines().map(|s| s.to_string());`, then filters/maps into `Vec&lt;TagName&gt;` and returns `Ok(...)` — there is no `if !output.success() { ... }` check anywhere in this function. Every sibling method in the same impl block does check: `head_sha` (lines 78-84), `commits_since` (lines 165-171), `create_tag` (lines 197-203), and `create_floating_major` (lines 218-224) all guard with `if !output.success() { return Err(VcsError::Git(...redact_git_stderr(&amp;output.stderr)...)) }`. (`resolve_commit` intentionally treats failure as `Ok(None)`, which is a documented, different design choice, not a silent-swallow bug.) So a failing `git tag --list` (e.g., not a git repo, corrupted repo) with empty/non-empty stderr and empty stdout falls through `stdout_lines()` on empty stdout and returns `Ok(vec![])` — indistinguishable from \"repository has zero tags\" — with no error surfaced and no stderr redaction/reporting path exercised at all.\n\n**Files read (absolute paths):**\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/membership.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-vcs/src/shell.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto-findings/FINDINGS.md` (checked — none of these three are already logged as CONFIRMED)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>53255</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>52544</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
