{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:30:30.829Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a89d89a4f8b66d2ae</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/a89d89a4f8b66d2ae.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify batch 1: identity/tag/validate bugs\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All three candidates investigated by reading current file content directly (not just diffs). Summary below.\n\n## Candidate 1 — `identity.rs::resolve_native_with_fallback` — REFUTED\n\n**Verdict: REFUTED** (as a bug — the mechanics described are real, but it's deliberate, tested behavior, not a defect).\n\nThe code at `crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs:466-490` does exactly what the claim describes mechanically: the `match candidates.len() { 0 =&gt; None, _ =&gt; { ...push diagnostic...; None } }` has no `1 =&gt; Some(...)` arm, unlike `resolve_native` (lines 436-440) which does. However, `git log` on this branch shows this was an intentional, deliberate fix, not drift:\n\n- `de83f6f5 fix(graph): stop resolve_native_with_fallback's silent single-candidate cross-ecosystem match (T13a)` — explicitly removes the `1 =&gt; Some(candidates[0].1)` arm and adds `resolve_native_with_fallback_returns_none_on_single_cross_ecosystem_candidate` (identity.rs:270), whose own test comment says \"a same-ecosystem miss must never silently fall back to a cross-ecosystem match.\"\n- `4ab9a283 test(graph): prove resolve_native_with_fallback suppression reaches real edge extraction and diagnostics end-to-end (T13b)` adds full end-to-end tests in `walk.rs` (`ac12_ac18_...`, `ac14_ac18_...`) proving the sole caller (`walk.rs:540-558`, `ManifestWalkResolver::build`) correctly does NOT create a `DepEdge` in this case and instead surfaces exactly one `UnknownPackage` Warning diagnostic naming the candidate ecosystem — this is asserted as the desired contract, referencing AC-12/AC-14/AC-15/AC-18 and `SPEC-TRACK3B1-IDENTITY-PROMOTION-CORE`.\n- `resolve_native` itself is untouched and still returns `Some` for a single cross-ecosystem candidate (pinned by `resolve_native_unchanged_still_returns_cross_ecosystem_match`, identity.rs:323), preserving the permissive contract for callers that don't have a diagnostics sink.\n\nSo the \"sole caller\" premise is also wrong — `walk.rs` relying on this behavior is exactly what the T13a/T13b commits added regression tests for, confirming the no-edge/diagnostic-only outcome is intended, not silently broken.\n\n## Candidate 2 — `commands/tag.rs::create_tags_with_options` floating-major sha asymmetry — CONFIRMED\n\n**Location**: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs:53-98` (dry-run/no-permit branch).\n\nIn the `let Some(permit) = permit else { ... }` (dry-run/preview) branch:\n- Floating-major block (lines 54-75): computes `let major_already_existed = ws.tags()?.contains_tag(major_tag.as_str());` (line 65) then unconditionally pushes `sha: release.sha.clone()` (line 69) regardless of `major_already_existed`.\n- Primary-tag block ~12 lines later, same branch (lines 77-89): computes `let already_existed = ws.tags()?.contains_tag(tag_str);` then does `let sha = if already_existed { match git.resolve_commit(tag_str)? { Some(actual) =&gt; actual, None =&gt; return Err(...) } } else { release.sha.clone() };` — correctly resolving the existing tag's real current sha in preview mode.\n\nThis is a real, uncorrected asymmetry: in preview mode, if the floating major tag (e.g. `v1`) already exists pointing at an old commit, the report will falsely claim its sha is the new release's sha, even though nothing was mutated. I checked `crates/callisto-graph/tests/tags_tests.rs` for coverage — every test exercising floating-major reporting (`floating_major: true`) passes `Some(&amp;ApplyPermit::force_for_tests())` (the apply path, where `release.sha` is correct because `create_floating_major` force-moves the tag there); no test exercises `permit: None` with `floating_major: true` and a pre-existing major tag, so this asymmetry is untested and unnoticed. The apply-path floating-major block (lines 117-141) is correctly symmetric with its own primary-tag handling, since the tag is genuinely force-moved to `release.sha` there — only the dry-run branch has the bug.\n\n## Candidate 3 — `validate.rs` vs `aggregate.rs` ambiguous-bare-name asymmetry — CONFIRMED\n\n**Locations**: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/validate.rs:100-118`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs:154-163` and `:271-291`.\n\n- `validate.rs:103`: `if !ws.graph.packages().any(|p| p.id.matches(&amp;id)) { ...push UnknownPackage Error... }` — uses `PackageId::matches` (`crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs:148-160`), whose ecosystem comparison is `(None, _) | (_, None) =&gt; true`. For a bare changeset entry name (parses to an ecosystem-less `PackageId`), `.any(...)` short-circuits to `true` on the *first* matching package regardless of ecosystem, so it can never detect that two different-ecosystem packages both matched — no ambiguity diagnostic is ever produced by `validate`.\n- `aggregate.rs:154-163` (`resolve_target_package`) uses `id.resolve_unique(packages, |p| &amp;p.id)`, mapping an ambiguous result to `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName { name, candidates })`. This is called at `aggregate.rs:291` (`match resolve_target_package(graph.packages(), &amp;id)? { ... }`) on the *same* parsed `PackageId` from the *same* changeset-entry loop that `validate.rs` walks — and it hard-errors via `?` on ambiguity, confirmed by tests `test_resolve_target_package_ambiguous_bare_name_errors` and `test_resolve_target_package_ambiguous_bare_name_three_ecosystems_errors` (aggregate.rs:1071, 1129).\n- I grepped `validate.rs` and its test files for any \"ambiguous\"/\"Ambiguous\" handling — none exists. So a changeset naming a bare `serde` (present as both a Cargo crate and an npm package) passes `callisto validate` silently, then fails downstream with `GraphError::AmbiguousName` the moment `aggregate()` (used by `plan-version`/`changelog`/etc.) processes the identical changeset — exactly the asymmetry claimed.\n\nRelevant files read in full/in part (all read-only, no edits made):\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/tags_tests.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/validate.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/ecosystem_bugs_test.rs` (via `git show de83f6f5`)\n\nNo findings written to the callisto-findings repo per instructions (not requested); reporting directly here as the read-only verification output.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>69993</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>27</tool_uses><duration_ms>144492</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:30:30.994Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a89d89a4f8b66d2ae</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/a89d89a4f8b66d2ae.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify batch 1: identity/tag/validate bugs\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All three candidates investigated by reading current file content directly (not just diffs). Summary below.\n\n## Candidate 1 — `identity.rs::resolve_native_with_fallback` — REFUTED\n\n**Verdict: REFUTED** (as a bug — the mechanics described are real, but it's deliberate, tested behavior, not a defect).\n\nThe code at `crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs:466-490` does exactly what the claim describes mechanically: the `match candidates.len() { 0 =&gt; None, _ =&gt; { ...push diagnostic...; None } }` has no `1 =&gt; Some(...)` arm, unlike `resolve_native` (lines 436-440) which does. However, `git log` on this branch shows this was an intentional, deliberate fix, not drift:\n\n- `de83f6f5 fix(graph): stop resolve_native_with_fallback's silent single-candidate cross-ecosystem match (T13a)` — explicitly removes the `1 =&gt; Some(candidates[0].1)` arm and adds `resolve_native_with_fallback_returns_none_on_single_cross_ecosystem_candidate` (identity.rs:270), whose own test comment says \"a same-ecosystem miss must never silently fall back to a cross-ecosystem match.\"\n- `4ab9a283 test(graph): prove resolve_native_with_fallback suppression reaches real edge extraction and diagnostics end-to-end (T13b)` adds full end-to-end tests in `walk.rs` (`ac12_ac18_...`, `ac14_ac18_...`) proving the sole caller (`walk.rs:540-558`, `ManifestWalkResolver::build`) correctly does NOT create a `DepEdge` in this case and instead surfaces exactly one `UnknownPackage` Warning diagnostic naming the candidate ecosystem — this is asserted as the desired contract, referencing AC-12/AC-14/AC-15/AC-18 and `SPEC-TRACK3B1-IDENTITY-PROMOTION-CORE`.\n- `resolve_native` itself is untouched and still returns `Some` for a single cross-ecosystem candidate (pinned by `resolve_native_unchanged_still_returns_cross_ecosystem_match`, identity.rs:323), preserving the permissive contract for callers that don't have a diagnostics sink.\n\nSo the \"sole caller\" premise is also wrong — `walk.rs` relying on this behavior is exactly what the T13a/T13b commits added regression tests for, confirming the no-edge/diagnostic-only outcome is intended, not silently broken.\n\n## Candidate 2 — `commands/tag.rs::create_tags_with_options` floating-major sha asymmetry — CONFIRMED\n\n**Location**: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs:53-98` (dry-run/no-permit branch).\n\nIn the `let Some(permit) = permit else { ... }` (dry-run/preview) branch:\n- Floating-major block (lines 54-75): computes `let major_already_existed = ws.tags()?.contains_tag(major_tag.as_str());` (line 65) then unconditionally pushes `sha: release.sha.clone()` (line 69) regardless of `major_already_existed`.\n- Primary-tag block ~12 lines later, same branch (lines 77-89): computes `let already_existed = ws.tags()?.contains_tag(tag_str);` then does `let sha = if already_existed { match git.resolve_commit(tag_str)? { Some(actual) =&gt; actual, None =&gt; return Err(...) } } else { release.sha.clone() };` — correctly resolving the existing tag's real current sha in preview mode.\n\nThis is a real, uncorrected asymmetry: in preview mode, if the floating major tag (e.g. `v1`) already exists pointing at an old commit, the report will falsely claim its sha is the new release's sha, even though nothing was mutated. I checked `crates/callisto-graph/tests/tags_tests.rs` for coverage — every test exercising floating-major reporting (`floating_major: true`) passes `Some(&amp;ApplyPermit::force_for_tests())` (the apply path, where `release.sha` is correct because `create_floating_major` force-moves the tag there); no test exercises `permit: None` with `floating_major: true` and a pre-existing major tag, so this asymmetry is untested and unnoticed. The apply-path floating-major block (lines 117-141) is correctly symmetric with its own primary-tag handling, since the tag is genuinely force-moved to `release.sha` there — only the dry-run branch has the bug.\n\n## Candidate 3 — `validate.rs` vs `aggregate.rs` ambiguous-bare-name asymmetry — CONFIRMED\n\n**Locations**: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/validate.rs:100-118`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs:154-163` and `:271-291`.\n\n- `validate.rs:103`: `if !ws.graph.packages().any(|p| p.id.matches(&amp;id)) { ...push UnknownPackage Error... }` — uses `PackageId::matches` (`crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs:148-160`), whose ecosystem comparison is `(None, _) | (_, None) =&gt; true`. For a bare changeset entry name (parses to an ecosystem-less `PackageId`), `.any(...)` short-circuits to `true` on the *first* matching package regardless of ecosystem, so it can never detect that two different-ecosystem packages both matched — no ambiguity diagnostic is ever produced by `validate`.\n- `aggregate.rs:154-163` (`resolve_target_package`) uses `id.resolve_unique(packages, |p| &amp;p.id)`, mapping an ambiguous result to `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName { name, candidates })`. This is called at `aggregate.rs:291` (`match resolve_target_package(graph.packages(), &amp;id)? { ... }`) on the *same* parsed `PackageId` from the *same* changeset-entry loop that `validate.rs` walks — and it hard-errors via `?` on ambiguity, confirmed by tests `test_resolve_target_package_ambiguous_bare_name_errors` and `test_resolve_target_package_ambiguous_bare_name_three_ecosystems_errors` (aggregate.rs:1071, 1129).\n- I grepped `validate.rs` and its test files for any \"ambiguous\"/\"Ambiguous\" handling — none exists. So a changeset naming a bare `serde` (present as both a Cargo crate and an npm package) passes `callisto validate` silently, then fails downstream with `GraphError::AmbiguousName` the moment `aggregate()` (used by `plan-version`/`changelog`/etc.) processes the identical changeset — exactly the asymmetry claimed.\n\nRelevant files read in full/in part (all read-only, no edits made):\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/tag.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/tags_tests.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/validate.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/ecosystem_bugs_test.rs` (via `git show de83f6f5`)\n\nNo findings written to the callisto-findings repo per instructions (not requested); reporting directly here as the read-only verification output.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>69993</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>27</tool_uses><duration_ms>144492</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:30:51.066Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac2fe2303e08bdb53</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ac2fe2303e08bdb53.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify batch 4: stale-key rewrite maintainability risk\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Verdict: CONFIRMED\n\nI read the full `ManifestWalkResolver::build` function in `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs` (branch `release-pipeline-and-matrix-hardening`), lines 79-430, not just the diff.\n\n**1. The numbered comments exist exactly as described.** `grep -n \"STALE-KEY\"` on the file returns:\n- `walk.rs:248` — `STALE-KEY REWRITE location (4): re-key package_manifest_decls under each path's own newly-promoted Prefixed id...`\n- `walk.rs:268` — `STALE-KEY REWRITE location (1): once a bare name is promoted, it MUST NOT remain in index.bare.`\n- `walk.rs:271` — `STALE-KEY REWRITE location (2): update index.native values for all native keys...`\n- `walk.rs:284` — `STALE-KEY REWRITE location (3)/(5): update index.prefixed values for the primary identities...`\n\nThat's 4 comment blocks covering location numbers 1 through 5 (3 and 5 are combined into one comment because `index.prefixed` needs the identical fix applied twice — once for `existing_path`, once for `rel_path`). The comment at line 248-254 also explicitly says this replaces \"exactly the AC-04 manifests-bleed bug,\" matching the candidate's claim verbatim.\n\n**2. Genuinely separate hand-synchronized patch sites, no shared helper.** Within the `if let Some((existing_path, existing_decls)) = package_manifest_decls.insert(...)` block (lines 230-309), each map is patched inline and independently: `package_manifest_decls` re-insert/remove (248-267), `index.bare.remove` (270), two `index.native.insert` loops (274-283), two `index.prefixed.insert` loops (287-304), and finally `promoted_siblings.entry(...).extend(...)` (305-308). There is no single \"re-key everything derived from this identity\" function — it's five distinct imperative update sites inside one large `build()` method, each commented by hand to explain why it can't be skipped.\n\n**3. No compiler- or test-enforced guarantee against a missed 6th location — and this has already actually happened.** `IdentityIndex` (`identity.rs:339-352`) has a 4th field, `platform: BTreeMap&lt;String, (PackageId, PathBuf, ManifestRole)&gt;`, populated earlier in the same per-path loop at `walk.rs:213-216` using `primary_id.clone()`. That insert is **not** among the STALE-KEY REWRITE sites — `grep` for `index.platform` shows it's written once and never re-keyed. So if a directory that later becomes one half of a cross-ecosystem promotion also contains an npm platform (napi) manifest, `index.platform`'s owner `PackageId` is left pointing at the pre-promotion bare id after promotion, while `index.bare`/`index.native`/`index.prefixed`/`package_manifest_decls` are all correctly updated. The existing test suite only covers this field in the single-directory `case_d_single_path_multi_ecosystem_retains_bare_id_and_preserves_platform` test (`walk.rs:1366`), which is not a cross-directory promotion case at all — I found no test exercising promotion + platform-manifest together. This is real, present-tense evidence that \"forgetting the 6th patch site\" isn't hypothetical; it already happened for the 4th field on the very struct this logic maintains, and nothing (type system or test suite) caught it.\n\nRelevant files read: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs` (lines 1-460, plus grep across the whole file for `STALE-KEY`, `index.`, `promoted_siblings.`, and promotion-related test names), `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs` (`IdentityIndex` struct definition, lines 339-352).\n\nNote: the `index.platform` staleness-on-promotion gap I found while tracing this is a plausible latent bug in its own right (untested, matches the same hazard class), but it is not yet in `FINDINGS.md` — flagging it here for the parent agent's awareness rather than logging it myself, since this task was scoped as read-only verification of the specific candidate.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>48080</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>135501</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
