{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T05:31:26.196Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac75eca2b6a87449a</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_015gP7BaBiZ7V6WCGBsWoGew</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ac75eca2b6a87449a.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Angle C: cross-file caller/callee tracer\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Audit summary\n\nI traced every public/pub(crate) signature or return-type change in the 10 requested files, grepped the whole workspace for callers, and read each call site against the new contract. Most changes in this PR are unusually well-executed: `resolve_package_config`'s `Option→Result` change, `aggregate`'s `runner→GitAccess` + `SeverityInference::infer`'s new `git` param, `IdentityIndex.platform`'s 2-tuple→3-tuple, `CascadeInput`'s new `tags`/`identity` fields, and `Manifest::persist` (write/persist split) are all threaded consistently through every real call site (`walk.rs`, `aggregate.rs`, `commands/version.rs`, `commands/snapshot.rs`, `commands/status.rs`, `apply.rs`). I specifically checked the one place that looked like a missed `persist()` call — `apply.rs`'s `CargoWorkspacePackage`/`CargoWorkspaceDependency` branches call `ws_res.write_version`/`write_dependency` with no following `.persist()` — but `WorkspaceCargoResolver` is a distinct type whose own `write_version`/`write_dependency` still call `self.persist()` internally, so that's correct, not a regression.\n\nThe one genuine issue I found is a stale/misleading diagnostic in a deliberately-changed function:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs\",\n    \"line\": 466,\n    \"summary\": \"resolve_native_with_fallback's doc comment and diagnostic message still describe 'ambiguity' (two-or-more), but the match arm was changed to `_ =&gt; {...}` so it now fires identically for exactly one cross-ecosystem candidate.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A polyglot workspace where an npm package depends by bare name on a crate that exists in exactly one other ecosystem (e.g. `some-lib` only as a Cargo package). Before this PR, walk.rs's ManifestWalkResolver::build (line ~540) would silently resolve this via the single-candidate fallback and create a DepEdge, so cascade/version-bump propagation worked. After this PR, resolve_native_with_fallback returns None for this exact case too (intentional per SPEC-TRACK3B2), so no edge is created — but the diagnostic pushed to the user says 'dependency name `some-lib` is ambiguous across ecosystems: cargo:some-lib; add an ecosystem prefix...' while listing only one candidate, which is factually wrong (not ambiguous, just cross-ecosystem) and will confuse anyone reading `callisto validate`/build diagnostics output. This is tested and intentional as a behavior change (test `resolve_native_with_fallback_returns_none_on_single_cross_ecosystem_candidate`), but the doc comment ('pushes a Diagnostic when cross-ecosystem ambiguity is detected (two packages...)') and the message text were not updated to match, and any workspace previously relying on implicit single-candidate cross-ecosystem resolution for dependency-graph edges will silently lose that edge (no error, just an easy-to-miss Warning-severity diagnostic).\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nEverything else I traced (resolve_package_config's new `GraphError::AmbiguousName` path, `PreMajorInferencePolicy` struct→enum, `PublishTarget::Npm.restricted→access`, `PackageIdParseError::LeadingHyphen` new variant, `matrix.rs`'s `parse_package_json`/`parse_pyproject_toml` split, `report.rs`'s field renames (`pr_body→body`, `valid→ok`, `created_tags→tags`) and new mandatory fields, `registry.rs`'s redaction helpers, `exec.rs`'s `run_quiet` default method, and `cascade.rs`'s `cascade_action` `source`-gated peer escalation) had every caller updated consistently, including the GitHub Action script (`action.yml`) which reads exit codes rather than the renamed JSON keys, so no cross-language break there either.\n\nNo files were modified — this was a read-only investigation.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>210543</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>83</tool_uses><duration_ms>904293</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
