{"parentUuid":"dd73c3fc-45b2-48c0-b4ee-dd94da8eebeb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"35b6619a-d515-4300-a461-744b9d7ddd67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RzbX6ujHXTjmFTBtBJEkBj","type":"tool_result","content":"130\t    /// Because `Bare(\"foo\")` matches *both* `Prefixed(Cargo, \"foo\")` and\n131\t    /// `Prefixed(Npm, \"foo\")`, a bare lookup in a graph that contains the\n132\t    /// same name in two or more ecosystems will produce multiple matches.\n133\t    /// Callers **must** collect all matches and surface an error when there\n134\t    /// are two or more — silently taking the first match is the ambiguity\n135\t    /// bug this function is designed to let callers detect.\n136\t    ///\n137\t    /// The canonical example is `resolve_target_package` in\n138\t    /// `callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`: it calls\n139\t    /// `packages.filter(|p| p.id.matches(id)).collect()`, then returns\n140\t    /// `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the result has two or more\n141\t    /// entries.  That pattern is the correct way to use `matches()` in any\n142\t    /// context that may encounter a polyglot workspace.\n143\t    ///\n144\t    /// Callers that only need a simple existence check (e.g. \"is there at\n145\t    /// least one package with this name?\") may use `.any()` safely — they\n146\t    /// rely on the aggregation layer to catch and reject ambiguous references\n147\t    /// before acting on them.\n148\t    pub fn matches(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n149\t        if self == other {\n150\t            return true;\n151\t        }\n152\t        if self.name() == other.name() {\n153\t            match (self.ecosystem(), other.ecosystem()) {\n154\t                (None, _) | (_, None) => true,\n155\t                (Some(e1), Some(e2)) => e1 == e2,\n156\t            }\n157\t        } else {\n158\t            false\n159\t        }\n160\t    }\n161\t\n162\t    /// The single implementation of the collect-and-error pattern\n163\t    /// [`matches`](Self::matches)'s own doc comment requires every caller\n164\t    /// to hand-roll: filters `items` down to those whose id (via `id_of`)\n165\t    /// matches `self`, then resolves the result to exactly one, none, or\n166\t    /// reports the ambiguity.\n167\t    ///\n168\t    /// - `Ok(None)`: no item matches.\n169\t    /// - `Ok(Some(item))`: exactly one item matches -- the unambiguous case.\n170\t    /// - `Err(candidates)`: two or more items match. The caller decides how\n171\t    ///   to report this (e.g. as a domain-specific \"ambiguous name\" error\n172\t    ///   naming `self` and `candidates`) -- this method stays generic over\n173\t    ///   any `T` (a `Package`, a bare `PackageId`, ...) rather than forcing\n174\t    ///   a particular error type on every layer that needs this check.\n175\t    pub fn resolve_unique<'a, T>(\n176\t        &self,\n177\t        items: impl Iterator<Item = &'a T>,\n178\t        id_of: impl Fn(&'a T) -> &'a PackageId,\n179\t    ) -> Result<Option<&'a T>, Vec<&'a T>> {\n180\t        let matching: Vec<&'a T> = items.filter(|item| id_of(item).matches(self)).collect();\n181\t        match matching.len() {\n182\t            0 => Ok(None),\n183\t            1 => Ok(Some(matching[0])),\n184\t            _ => Err(matching),\n185\t        }\n186\t    }\n187\t}\n188\t\n189\t/// Trait for package identity resolution across ecosystem boundaries.\n190\tpub trait PackageIdentityResolver {\n191\t    /// Returns true if two package IDs refer to the same logical package.\n192\t    fn matches_id(&self, other: &PackageId) -> bool;\n193\t}\n194\t\n195\timpl PackageIdentityResolver for PackageId {\n196\t    fn matches_id(&self, other: &PackageId) -> bool {\n197\t        self.matches(other)\n198\t    }\n199\t}"}]},"uuid":"3ea21a12-f635-4966-a5af-ae5f733dedc1","timestamp":"2026-08-21T13:03:49.144Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs","content":"    /// Because `Bare(\"foo\")` matches *both* `Prefixed(Cargo, \"foo\")` and\n    /// `Prefixed(Npm, \"foo\")`, a bare lookup in a graph that contains the\n    /// same name in two or more ecosystems will produce multiple matches.\n    /// Callers **must** collect all matches and surface an error when there\n    /// are two or more — silently taking the first match is the ambiguity\n    /// bug this function is designed to let callers detect.\n    ///\n    /// The canonical example is `resolve_target_package` in\n    /// `callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`: it calls\n    /// `packages.filter(|p| p.id.matches(id)).collect()`, then returns\n    /// `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the result has two or more\n    /// entries.  That pattern is the correct way to use `matches()` in any\n    /// context that may encounter a polyglot workspace.\n    ///\n    /// Callers that only need a simple existence check (e.g. \"is there at\n    /// least one package with this name?\") may use `.any()` safely — they\n    /// rely on the aggregation layer to catch and reject ambiguous references\n    /// before acting on them.\n    pub fn matches(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n        if self == other {\n            return true;\n        }\n        if self.name() == other.name() {\n            match (self.ecosystem(), other.ecosystem()) {\n                (None, _) | (_, None) => true,\n                (Some(e1), Some(e2)) => e1 == e2,\n            }\n        } else {\n            false\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The single implementation of the collect-and-error pattern\n    /// [`matches`](Self::matches)'s own doc comment requires every caller\n    /// to hand-roll: filters `items` down to those whose id (via `id_of`)\n    /// matches `self`, then resolves the result to exactly one, none, or\n    /// reports the ambiguity.\n    ///\n    /// - `Ok(None)`: no item matches.\n    /// - `Ok(Some(item))`: exactly one item matches -- the unambiguous case.\n    /// - `Err(candidates)`: two or more items match. The caller decides how\n    ///   to report this (e.g. as a domain-specific \"ambiguous name\" error\n    ///   naming `self` and `candidates`) -- this method stays generic over\n    ///   any `T` (a `Package`, a bare `PackageId`, ...) rather than forcing\n    ///   a particular error type on every layer that needs this check.\n    pub fn resolve_unique<'a, T>(\n        &self,\n        items: impl Iterator<Item = &'a T>,\n        id_of: impl Fn(&'a T) -> &'a PackageId,\n    ) -> Result<Option<&'a T>, Vec<&'a T>> {\n        let matching: Vec<&'a T> = items.filter(|item| id_of(item).matches(self)).collect();\n        match matching.len() {\n            0 => Ok(None),\n            1 => Ok(Some(matching[0])),\n            _ => Err(matching),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Trait for package identity resolution across ecosystem boundaries.\npub trait PackageIdentityResolver {\n    /// Returns true if two package IDs refer to the same logical package.\n    fn matches_id(&self, other: &PackageId) -> bool;\n}\n\nimpl PackageIdentityResolver for PackageId {\n    fn matches_id(&self, other: &PackageId) -> bool {\n        self.matches(other)\n    }\n}","numLines":70,"startLine":130,"totalLines":564}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dd73c3fc-45b2-48c0-b4ee-dd94da8eebeb","session_id":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.238","gitBranch":"release-pipeline-and-matrix-hardening","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
